	"common.back": "Back",
	"common.continue": "Continue",
	"common.cost": "(Cost: $COST$ PC)",
	"common.next": "Next",
	"common.page_of": "$N$ / $TOTAL$",
	"common.previous": "Previous",
	"common.unavailable": "UNAVAILABLE",
	"config.difficulty.easy.desc": "A version of that year where things did not quite go the way everyone remembers. The Palace begins in a somewhat sturdier position than history is generally willing to admit, the old regime's lingering hooks into the state a bit looser than usual, and [color=__STAT_PC__][b]the consequences of a misstep slower to arrive and gentler when they do.[/b][/color] It is still 1999. It is just, for once, a somewhat kinder one. \n\n• Significantly higher starting [color=__STAT_PC__][b]Political Capital[/b][/color] and a steady passive tailwind. \n\n• Early national metrics and faction stances are significantly boosted. \n\n• Starting [color=__STAT_CENDANA__][b]Cendana Influence[/b][/color] is $tier.cendana.contained$, and it never claws its way back. \n\n• There are no passive [color=__CLR_FIREBRICK__][b]maluses[/b][/color] on faction loyalty. \n\n• Actions and decrees cost noticeably less, and faction outreach pays better. Up to four favors can be banked. \n\n• Bad news from events lands softer, good news a little stronger, and the support you build does not fade. \n\n• The army and the old regime grumble, but rarely do more than that, and few delegates break ranks in the secret ballot.",
	"config.difficulty.easy.name": "Easy",
	"config.difficulty.high.desc": "[color=__STAT_PC__][b]You inherit a position that owes very little to luck and even less to generosity.[/b][/color] With the old regime's fingerprints still very much present in places the Palace would rather they weren't. Left unattended, certain problems have a way of making other problems worse. Beyond that, it is probably better left for you to find out for yourself, [color=__CLR_FIREBRICK__][b]the way Habibie did.[/b][/color]\n\n• Lower starting [color=__STAT_PC__][b]Political Capital.[/b][/color]\n\n• Starting [color=__STAT_CENDANA__][b]Cendana Influence[/b][/color] is deeply $tier.cendana.entrenched$, and slowly rebuilds if you cut it down. \n\n• Passive [color=__CLR_FIREBRICK__][b]maluses[/b][/color] bite hard if stability falters. \n\n• Actions and decrees cost somewhat more, and faction outreach pays slightly less. \n\n• Bad news from events hits harder than it should. \n\n• Support you have built fades unless it is maintained, and delegates are likelier to break ranks in the secret ballot. \n\n• [color=__CLR_FIREBRICK__][b]Alienate the generals[/b][/color] and expect sabotage in the streets and in East Timor.",
	"config.difficulty.high.name": "High",
	"config.difficulty.historical.desc": "[color=__CLR_FIREBRICK__][b]What really went down that year.[/b][/color] The old regime's grip on the state has barely loosened, and the problems Suharto left unattended have an unpleasant tendency to compound into one another before you fully notice it happening. Even the ordinary business of governing costs more than it should. [b]RECOMMENDED WAY TO PLAY.[/b] \n\n• Scarce starting [color=__STAT_PC__][b]Political Capital.[/b][/color]\n\n• Starting [color=__STAT_CENDANA__][b]Cendana Influence[/b][/color] is $tier.cendana.untouchable$, and they recover influence faster. \n\n• Passive [color=__CLR_FIREBRICK__][b]maluses[/b][/color] are unforgiving when the country destabilizes. \n\n• Every political action costs more than it should, routine business needs longer to recover, and faction negotiations buys loyalty. Only two favors can be banked. \n\n• Events cut deeper than they heal. \n\n• Delegates defect far more readily. \n\n• An [color=__CLR_FIREBRICK__][b]$tier.mil.alienated$[/b][/color] military will do whatever they can to oust you.",
	"config.difficulty.historical.name": "Historical",
	"config.difficulty.medium.desc": "Something closer to the middle ground between what actually happened and what might have happened under slightly better circumstances. [color=__STAT_PC__][b]You inherit a position that is neither generous nor especially cruel.[/b][/color] The administration is still tangled up in the old regime's entanglements to a degree that will make itself known eventually. \n\n• Moderate starting [color=__STAT_PC__][b]Political Capital[/b][/color] and a slight passive tailwind. \n\n• Early national metrics are modestly boosted. \n\n• Starting [color=__STAT_CENDANA__][b]Cendana Influence[/b][/color] is $tier.cendana.subversive$. \n\n• Passive [color=__CLR_FIREBRICK__][b]maluses[/b][/color] are mild and manageable. \n\n• Standard action costs and standard faction outreach. Up to three favors can be banked. \n\n• Support built far above a faction's starting stance fades very slowly if you ignore it. \n\n• [color=__CLR_FIREBRICK__][b]An alienated military[/b][/color] will push back some of the time.",
	"config.difficulty.medium.name": "Medium",
	"config.difficulty.ucn.desc": "[color=__CLR_FIREBRICK__][b]You survived the real thing. Now you choose how bad it gets.[/b][/color] Twenty-two dials, each from 0 to 20, control every handicap in the game: your opening hand, the price of governing, how much good news is worth, how loyal anyone stays, and how the generals and the Assembly behave on the final night. Ten on every dial is roughly Historical. \n\n• Every starting metric, every faction's goodwill and the cost of every action can be tuned separately. \n\n• Presets: all 0, all 10, all 20, or let fate decide. \n\n• Settings are locked in when the campaign starts and travel with your save. \n\n• [color=__CLR_FIREBRICK__][b]Win with every dial at 20 and the night ends at 6 AM.[/b][/color]",
	"config.difficulty.ucn.name": "Severe State Situations",
	"credits.body.images": "[b]Placeholder Image:[/b] @Dadcho1 on x, [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://x.com/Dadcho1/status/2113243112940405176?s=20]https://x.com/Dadcho1/status/2113243112940405176?s=20[/url][/color] \n\n[b]Majalah TEMPO & Pusat Data dan Analisa Tempo (PDAT):[/b] Archival photojournalism of the May 1998 riots, the Trisakti and Semanggi student protests, B.J. Habibie's cabinet, the June 1999 legislative elections, and the October 1999 MPR General Session.\n\n[b]LKBN Antara (Antara Foto):[/b] Official state wire photographs of Merdeka Palace, Bina Graha, the DPR/MPR Senayan complex, and regional visits.\n\n[b]Harian Kompas, Majalah Gatra, Forum Keadilan, Republika & The Jakarta Post:[/b] Contemporary 1998–1999 Indonesian press photography and editorial archives.\n\n[b]International Wire Services (Associated Press / AP, Reuters & Agence France-Presse / AFP):[/b] Press photographs covering the Asian Financial Crisis, IMF negotiations, the 1999 East Timor UNAMET ballot, and Jakarta street demonstrations.\n\n[b]Arsip Nasional Republik Indonesia (ANRI), Perpustakaan Nasional RI & Wikimedia Commons:[/b] Public domain state portraits, parliamentary emblems, and party insignia.\n\n[i][b]Archival & Fair Use Notice:[/b] Historical photographs and press images used in this project were gathered via public Google Search from online press archives and historical repositories. All photographs remain the property of their respective photojournalists and media publications (including TEMPO, Kompas, Antara, and international wire agencies) and are presented here solely for non-commercial, educational, and historical documentation purposes.[/i]",
	"credits.body.main": "[b]Created, Designed, Written & Programmed by[/b]\nMeniir\n\n────────────────────\n[b][color=__CLR_DARK_RED__]IN MEMORIAM[/color][/b]\n[ PHOTO: IN MEMORIAM ]\n\nIn memory of the four unarmed Trisakti University students shot dead with live ammunition inside their own campus gates on May 12, 1998: [b]Elang Mulia Lesmana[/b], [b]Hery Hertanto[/b], [b]Hafidin Royan[/b], and [b]Hendriawan Sie[/b].\n\nIn memory of the more than [b]1,190 human beings[/b] burned alive, shot, or beaten to death across Jakarta, Surakarta, Medan, and Palembang over three days in May 1998—hundreds of whom, including urban poor and children, were trapped and incinerated inside shopping malls such as [b]Yogya Plaza Klender[/b] after the exits were locked and doused with fuel.\n\nIn recognition of the [b]hundreds of ethnic Chinese-Indonesian women and girls[/b]—at least 85 to nearly 400 documented by the [i]Tim Relawan untuk Kemanusiaan[/i] and the Joint Fact-Finding Team (TGPF)—who were systematically hunted down in their homes, shops, and streets across Glodok, Pluit, Jembatan Lima, and Tanah Abang, gang-raped in front of their families, sexually mutilated with razor blades and broken glass, and thrown into burning buildings. And in memory of [b]Ita Martadinata Haryono[/b] (18), a Chinese-Indonesian high school student, survivor, and volunteer counselor who was brutally stabbed to death and had her throat slit in her Jakarta bedroom on October 9, 1998—just days before she was scheduled to fly to New York to testify before the United Nations about the May 1998 gang rapes. Not a single organizer, commander, or perpetrator was ever convicted.\n\nIn memory of labor organizer [b]Marsinah[/b] (24), abducted, raped, tortured, and murdered in East Java in May 1993; journalist [b]Fuad Muhammad Syafruddin ('Udin')[/b] of [i]Harian Bernas[/i], beaten to death in Yogyakarta in August 1996; and the [b]13 pro-democracy activists abducted in 1997–1998[/b] who were forcibly disappeared and never returned to their families: poet [b]Wiji Thukul[/b], [b]Bimo Petrus Anugerah[/b], [b]Herman Hendrawan[/b], [b]Suyat[/b], [b]Yani Afri[/b], [b]Sonny[/b], [b]Dedi Hamdun[/b], [b]Noval Alkatiri[/b], [b]Ismail[/b], [b]Ucok Munandar Siahaan[/b], [b]Hendra Hambali[/b], [b]Yadin Muhidin[/b], and [b]Abdun Nasser[/b].\n\nIn memory of the 18 students, schoolchildren, and citizens killed when security forces fired live rounds into crowds protesting the 1998 MPR Special Session along Jalan Sudirman and the Atma Jaya campus: [b]Bernardinus Realino Norma Irmawan ('Wawan')[/b] (20, Atma Jaya student shot in the chest while carrying a wounded classmate), [b]Teddy Wardhani Kusuma[/b] (ITI), [b]Sigit Prasetyo[/b] (YAI), [b]Heru Sudibyo[/b], [b]Engkus Kusnadi[/b] (Universitas Jakarta), [b]Muzammil Joko[/b] (Universitas Indonesia), high school student [b]Lukman Firdaus[/b], 6-year-old [b]Ayu Ratna Sari[/b] (killed by a stray bullet to the head), [b]Uga Usmana[/b], [b]Abdullah / Donit[/b], [b]Agus Setiana[/b], [b]Budiono[/b], [b]Doni Effendi[/b], [b]Rinanto[/b], [b]Sidik[/b], [b]Kristian Nikijulong[/b], and [b]Hadi[/b].\n\nIn memory of the students and citizens shot dead in Jakarta, Lampung, and Palembang while protesting the State of Danger Bill (RUU PKB) on the eve of the 1999 MPR General Session: [b]Yap Yun Hap[/b] (21, Universitas Indonesia electrical engineering student shot dead at Semanggi), [b]Zainal Abidin[/b], [b]Teja Sukmana[/b], [b]M. Nuh Ichsan[/b], [b]Salim Jumadoi[/b], [b]Fadly[/b], [b]Deny Julian[/b], [b]Yusuf Rizal[/b], [b]Saidatul Fitria[/b] (Universitas Lampung), and [b]Meyer Ardiansyah[/b] (IBA Palembang).\n\nIn memory of the thousands of civilians tortured, raped, and executed during the decade of [i]Daerah Operasi Militer[/i] (DOM) in [b]Aceh[/b], the 46 civilians gunned down at the [b]Simpang KKA Massacre[/b] in North Aceh (May 3, 1999), and the 57 villagers—including [b]Teungku Bantaqiah[/b] and his students—massacred and dumped into mass graves in [b]Beutong Ateuh[/b] (July 23, 1999). In memory of the more than [b]1,400 East Timorese civilians[/b] slaughtered by military-backed militias in 1999 at the [b]Liquiça Church[/b], the home of [b]Manuel Carrascalão[/b], the [b]Suai Church[/b], Maliana, and Dili, alongside murdered journalists [b]Agus Muliawan[/b] and [b]Sander Thoenes[/b]. And in memory of the thousands of civilians killed and displaced in the communal violence in [b]Ambon/Maluku[/b] and [b]Sambas, West Kalimantan[/b].\n\n[color=__CLR_DARK_RED__][b]We Refuse to Forget.[/b][/color]",
	"credits.body.music": "[b][color=__CLR_NAVY__]NATIONAL & PATRIOTIC ANTHEMS[/color][/b]\n\n[b]Indonesia Raya (Instrumental):[/b] Composed by Wage Rudolf Supratman\n\n[b]Tanah Airku:[/b] Written and composed by Ibu Sud (Saridjah Niung)\n\n[b]Sorak-Sorak Bergembira:[/b] Written and composed by Cornel Simanjuntak\n\n[b]Tinggalkan Ayah Tinggalkan Ibu:[/b] Mars Militer TNI (Composed by Ibu Sud / Traditional)\n\n[b]Bapak Pembangunan Indonesia:[/b] Written and performed by Titiek Puspa\n\n────────────────────\n[b][color=__CLR_NAVY__]SOCIAL CRITIQUE & REFORMASI ANTHEMS[/color][/b]\n\n[b]Bongkar:[/b] Written by Iwan Fals & Sawung Jabo, performed by SWAMI / Iwan Fals\n\n[b]Bento:[/b] Written by Iwan Fals & Naniel Yakin, performed by SWAMI / Iwan Fals\n\n[b]Pesawat Tempurku:[/b] Written and performed by Iwan Fals\n\n[b]Serdadu:[/b] Written and performed by Iwan Fals\n\n[b]Bangsa Bayar Hutang:[/b] Written and performed by Franky Sahilatua\n\n[b]Berita Cuaca:[/b] Written and performed by Gombloh\n\n[b]Tante Sun:[/b] Written by Sam Bimbo, Jaka Bimbo & Iwan Abdurahman, performed by Bimbo\n\n[b]Buruh Tani Mahasiswa (Pembebasan):[/b] Written and composed by Safi'i Kemamang\n\n[b]Mars Mahasiswa (Totalitas Perjuangan):[/b] Indonesian Student Movement Anthem (Traditional / Anonymous)\n\n────────────────────\n[b][color=__CLR_NAVY__]INDONESIAN POP (NUSANTARA)[/color][/b]\n\n[b]Cintaku:[/b] Written by Eros Djarot & Debby Nasution, performed by Chrisye\n\n[b]Hip Hip Hura:[/b] Written by Adjie Soetama, performed by Chrisye\n\n[b]Anak Sekolah:[/b] Written by Oddie Agam, performed by Chrisye\n\n[b]Kala Cinta Menggoda:[/b] Written by Guruh Sukarnoputra, performed by Chrisye\n\n[b]Sakura:[/b] Written by Fariz RM & Jimmy Paais, performed by Fariz RM\n\n[b]Barcelona:[/b] Written and performed by Fariz RM\n\n[b]Kangen:[/b] Written by Ahmad Dhani, performed by Dewa 19\n\n[b]Kirana:[/b] Written by Ahmad Dhani & Erwin Prasetya, performed by Dewa 19\n\n[b]Kamulah Satu-Satunya:[/b] Written by Ahmad Dhani & Erwin Prasetya, performed by Dewa 19\n\n[b]Bagaikan Langit:[/b] Written by Melly Goeslaw, performed by Potret\n\n[b]Sobat:[/b] Written by Piyu, Ari & Rindra, performed by Padi\n\n[b]Begitu Indah:[/b] Written by Piyu, performed by Padi\n\n[b]Mahadewi:[/b] Written by Piyu & Fadly, performed by Padi\n\n[b]Dan...:[/b] Written by Eross Candra, performed by Sheila on 7\n\n[b]Kita:[/b] Written by Eross Candra, performed by Sheila on 7\n\n────────────────────\n[b][color=__CLR_NAVY__]INTERNATIONAL[/color][/b]\n\n[b]漫步人生路 (Man Bu Ren Sheng Lu):[/b] Composed by Miyuki Nakajima, lyrics by Cheng Kwok-kong, performed by Teresa Teng\n\n[b]海闊天空 (Boundless Oceans, Vast Skies):[/b] Written by Wong Ka Kui, performed by Beyond\n\n[b]Everybody Wants to Rule the World:[/b] Written by Roland Orzabal, Ian Stanley & Chris Hughes, performed by Tears for Fears\n\n[b]I Want It That Way:[/b] Written by Max Martin & Andreas Carlsson, performed by Backstreet Boys\n\n[b]Smooth:[/b] Written by Itaal Shur & Rob Thomas, performed by Santana feat. Rob Thomas",
	"credits.body.sfx": "[b]Click:[/b] Sanedish, [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://freesound.org/people/Sanedish/sounds/831995/]https://freesound.org/people/Sanedish/sounds/831995/[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Notification:[/b] HenryCena82595, [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://freesound.org/people/HenryCena82595/sounds/731783/]https://freesound.org/people/HenryCena82595/sounds/731783/[/url][/color]\n\n[b]News:[/b] alexarje, [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://freesound.org/people/alexarje/sounds/863259/]https://freesound.org/people/alexarje/sounds/863259/[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Achievement Unlocked:[/b] canberries4, [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://freesound.org/people/canberries4/sounds/866244/]https://freesound.org/people/canberries4/sounds/866244/[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Action Blocked:[/b] LilMati, [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://freesound.org/people/LilMati/sounds/658266/]https://freesound.org/people/LilMati/sounds/658266/[/url][/color]",
	"credits.body.sources": "[b]Indonesian Politics and Society: A Reader[/b] by David Bourchier and Vedi R. Hadiz\n\n[b]B.J. Habibie[/b], [i]Detik-Detik yang Menentukan: Jalan Panjang Indonesia Menuju Demokrasi[/i] (Jakarta: THC Mandiri, 2006)\n\n[b]Report on Indonesia's 7 June 1999 Parliamentary General Election and Recommendations for Electoral Reform[/b] by the International Foundation for Electoral Systems: [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://www.ifes.org/publications/report-indonesias-7-june-1999-parliamentary-general-election-and-recommendations]https://www.ifes.org/publications/report-indonesias-7-june-1999-parliamentary-general-election-and-recommendations[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Back to the Barracks: Getting the Indonesian Military Out of Politics, 1998-2000[/b] by Tristan Dreisbach: [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://successfulsocieties.princeton.edu/publications/back-barracks-getting-indonesian-military-out-politics-1998-2000]https://successfulsocieties.princeton.edu/publications/back-barracks-getting-indonesian-military-out-politics-1998-2000[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Indonesian Politics and the Military 1997-1999: From Repression to Reform?[/b] by Ronald D. Palmer: [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://ciaotest.cc.columbia.edu/olj/ad/ad_v5_2/par01.html]https://ciaotest.cc.columbia.edu/olj/ad/ad_v5_2/par01.html[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Wikipedia — B. J. Habibie:[/b] [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/B._J._Habibie]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/B._J._Habibie[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Wikipedia — Development Reform Cabinet (Kabinet Reformasi Pembangunan):[/b] [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Development_Reform_Cabinet]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Development_Reform_Cabinet[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Wikipedia — Fall of Suharto:[/b] [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fall_of_Suharto]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fall_of_Suharto[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Wikipedia — Post-Suharto Era in Indonesia (Reformasi):[/b] [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Post-Suharto_era_in_Indonesia]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Post-Suharto_era_in_Indonesia[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Wikipedia — New Order (Indonesia):[/b] [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Order_(Indonesia)]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Order_(Indonesia)[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Wikipedia — 1999 Indonesian Legislative Election:[/b] [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1999_Indonesian_legislative_election]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1999_Indonesian_legislative_election[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Wikipedia — 1999 Indonesian Presidential Election (MPR General Session):[/b] [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1999_Indonesian_presidential_election]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1999_Indonesian_presidential_election[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Wikipedia — Sidang Istimewa MPR (1998 & 1999 MPR Sessions):[/b] [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://id.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sidang_Istimewa_MPR]https://id.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sidang_Istimewa_MPR[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Wikipedia — People's Consultative Assembly (MPR) & House of Representatives (DPR):[/b] [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/People%27s_Consultative_Assembly]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/People%27s_Consultative_Assembly[/url][/color] | [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Representatives_(Indonesia)]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Representatives_(Indonesia)[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Wikipedia — Poros Tengah (The Central Axis):[/b] [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://id.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poros_Tengah]https://id.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poros_Tengah[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Wikipedia — Dwifungsi (ABRI Dual Function Doctrine):[/b] [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dwifungsi]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dwifungsi[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Wikipedia — 1997 Asian Financial Crisis (Krismon):[/b] [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1997_Asian_financial_crisis]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1997_Asian_financial_crisis[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Wikipedia — Bank Bali Scandal ('Baligate'):[/b] [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bank_Bali_scandal]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bank_Bali_scandal[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Wikipedia — Bank Indonesia Liquidity Support (BLBI) & IBRA (BPPN):[/b] [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bank_Indonesia_Liquidity_Support]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bank_Indonesia_Liquidity_Support[/url][/color] | [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indonesian_Bank_Restructuring_Agency]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indonesian_Bank_Restructuring_Agency[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Wikipedia — IPTN N250 (Gatotkaca):[/b] [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IPTN_N250]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IPTN_N250[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Wikipedia — Trisakti Shootings (May 12, 1998):[/b] [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trisakti_shootings]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trisakti_shootings[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Wikipedia — May 1998 Riots of Indonesia:[/b] [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/May_1998_riots_of_Indonesia]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/May_1998_riots_of_Indonesia[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Wikipedia — Semanggi Shootings (Semanggi I & II):[/b] [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Semanggi_shootings]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Semanggi_shootings[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Wikipedia — 1997–98 Activists Kidnappings in Indonesia (Tim Mawar):[/b] [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1997%E2%80%9398_activists_kidnappings_in_Indonesia]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1997%E2%80%9398_activists_kidnappings_in_Indonesia[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Wikipedia — 27 July 1996 Incident (Peristiwa Kudatuli):[/b] [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/27_July_1996_incident]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/27_July_1996_incident[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Wikipedia — 1998 East Java Ninja Scare (Banyuwangi Killings):[/b] [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1998_East_Java_ninja_scare]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1998_East_Java_ninja_scare[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Wikipedia — 1999 East Timorese Independence Referendum & 1999 Crisis:[/b] [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1999_East_Timorese_independence_referendum]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1999_East_Timorese_independence_referendum[/url][/color] | [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1999_East_Timorese_crisis]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1999_East_Timorese_crisis[/url][/color]\n\n[b]Wikipedia — Insurgency in Aceh, Maluku Sectarian Conflict & Sambas Riots:[/b] [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Insurgency_in_Aceh]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Insurgency_in_Aceh[/url][/color] | [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maluku_sectarian_conflict]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maluku_sectarian_conflict[/url][/color] | [color=__STAT_PC__][url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sambas_riots]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sambas_riots[/url][/color]",
