	"gov.precarious": "PRECARIOUS",
	"gov.secure": "SECURE",
	"gov.stable": "STABLE",
	"gov.unstable": "UNSTABLE",
	"horse.btn_bloc": "Midnight Dinner with a few delegates | [color=__STAT_PC__]$COST$ PC[/color]",
	"horse.btn_single": "Talk to a delegate | [color=__STAT_PC__]$COST$ PC[/color]",
	"horse.exhausted": "Our Political Capital is exhausted. The floor whips have nothing left to trade tonight.",
	"horse.maxed": "$NAME$ is already behind the Palace.",
	"horse.stance_line": "[b]$SEATS$ Delegates[/b] | Disposition: $STANCE$",
	"horse.title": "Night of the Long Knives",
	"hud.gov_short": "GOV",
	"hud.government_stability": "GOVERNMENT STABILITY",
	"hud.pc_short": "PC",
	"hud.political_capital": "POLITICAL CAPITAL",
	"hud.turn_label": "TURN:",
	"hud.turn_short": "T",
	"language.unavailable_tooltip": "$NAME$ is unavailable.",
	"leverage.absolute.desc": "Party brokers come hat in hand; concessions come cheap.",
	"leverage.absolute.name": "Commanding",
	"leverage.default.desc": "Standard backroom terms",
	"leverage.moderate.desc": "Talks proceed on equal footing with standard political give-and-take",
	"leverage.moderate.name": "Balanced",
	"leverage.nonexistent.desc": "The Palace is negotiating from its knees; every overture is extortionate.",
	"leverage.nonexistent.name": "None",
	"leverage.strong.desc": "Bina Graha holds the upper hand at the negotiating table",
	"leverage.strong.name": "Strong",
	"leverage.weak.desc": "Caucus leaders sense blood in the water and demand steeper concessions",
	"leverage.weak.name": "Weak",
	"lib.lib_000.desc": "Under the unamended 1945 Constitution in effect in 1999, the MPR is the highest constitutional institution in Indonesia and the sole body empowered to elect the President and Vice President. In October 1999, it comprises 700 delegates: 462 elected members of the DPR (parliament), 38 appointed military and police officers ([i]Fraksi ABRI[/i]), 135 regional representatives elected by provincial legislatures ([i]Utusan Daerah[/i]), and 65 functional delegates from civic, religious, labor, and academic associations ([i]Utusan Golongan[/i]).",
	"lib.lib_000.subtitle": "People's Consultative Assembly: The 700-Seat Supreme Constitutional Body",
	"lib.lib_000.term": "MPR (Majelis Permusyawaratan Rakyat)",
	"lib.lib_001.desc": "Indonesia's primary legislative chamber, seated at the Senayan parliamentary complex in Jakarta. Following the June 7, 1999 election, 462 of its 500 seats are filled by elected party members and 38 are reserved for appointed ABRI officers. All 500 members of the DPR automatically sit as delegates inside the 700-seat MPR.",
	"lib.lib_001.subtitle": "House of Representatives: The 500-Seat National Legislature",
	"lib.lib_001.term": "DPR (Dewan Perwakilan Rakyat)",
	"lib.lib_002.desc": "Located at Jalan Veteran No. 16 on the northern edge of the Merdeka Palace complex, Bina Graha served throughout the New Order and the Habibie presidency as the working headquarters of the President and cabinet. The State Secretariat ([i]Sekneg[/i]), headed under Habibie by Akbar Tandjung and later Muladi, controlled the flow of presidential decrees, cabinet access, and off-budget operational funds.",
	"lib.lib_002.subtitle": "The Presidential Executive Office & State Secretariat",
	"lib.lib_002.term": "Bina Graha & Sekretariat Negara (Sekneg)",
	"lib.lib_003.desc": "Established by President Habibie in 1999 under Law No. 3/1999 to replace the New Order's state-controlled election board (LPU). Composed of representatives from the 48 contesting political parties alongside 5 government delegates (chaired by Gen. (Ret.) Rudini), the KPU oversaw the June 7, 1999 legislative election. Deadlocks inside the commission delayed the final certification of results until President Habibie intervened by decree.",
	"lib.lib_003.subtitle": "General Elections Commission",
	"lib.lib_003.term": "KPU (Komisi Pemilihan Umum)",
	"lib.lib_004.desc": "The constitutional state auditing body responsible for examining the management of public finances, chaired in 1998-1999 by Satrio Budihardjo ('Billy') Joedono. In August and September 1999, the BPK and international accounting firm PricewaterhouseCoopers (PwC) conducted the forensic audit of the Rp 546 billion Bank Bali transfer, exposing campaign-finance links to senior Palace and Golkar insiders.",
	"lib.lib_004.subtitle": "Supreme Audit Agency of the Republic of Indonesia",
	"lib.lib_004.term": "BPK (Badan Pemeriksa Keuangan)",
	"lib.lib_005.desc": "Indonesia's public prosecution service, headquartered in Kebayoran Baru, South Jakarta. In 1998-1999, under Attorney General Lt. Gen. Andi Muhammad Ghalib and later Acting Attorney General Ismudjoko, the office stood at the center of public controversy over its deferential investigation into former President Suharto's wealth and the Bank Bali scandal.",
	"lib.lib_005.subtitle": "Attorney General's Office of the Republic of Indonesia",
	"lib.lib_005.term": "Kejaksaan Agung (Kejagung)",
	"lib.lib_006.desc": "The highest judicial court in Indonesia. Under the New Order, the judiciary was subordinated to the executive branch through the Ministry of Justice, which controlled judges' pay, promotions, and transfers. Reforming the Supreme Court and transferring administrative control of all courts to the MA ('One-Roof' / [i]Satu Atap[/i] reform under Law No. 35/1999) was a key demand of legal reformers.",
	"lib.lib_006.subtitle": "Supreme Court of the Republic of Indonesia",
	"lib.lib_006.term": "Mahkamah Agung (MA)",
	"lib.lib_007.desc": "The technocratic nerve center of Indonesian macroeconomic policy and foreign aid coordination. On May 20, 1998, it was inside the Bappenas building that fourteen economic ministers led by Ginandjar Kartasasmita signed the historic letter refusing to serve in Suharto's reshuffled cabinet, sealing Suharto's resignation the next morning.",
	"lib.lib_007.subtitle": "National Development Planning Agency",
	"lib.lib_007.term": "Bappenas (Badan Perencanaan Pembangunan Nasional)",
	"lib.lib_008.desc": "Originally established by presidential decree in 1993 and granted statutory subpoena powers under Habibie's Law No. 39/1999 on Human Rights. Under figures like Marzuki Darusman, Djoko Sugianto, and Albert Hasibuan, Komnas HAM formed special investigative commissions (KPP-HAM) into the May 1998 riots, the Trisakti and Semanggi shootings, DOM atrocities in Aceh, and the 1999 East Timor violence.",
	"lib.lib_008.subtitle": "Komisi Nasional Hak Asasi Manusia (National Commission on Human Rights)",
	"lib.lib_008.term": "Komnas HAM",
	"lib.lib_009.desc": "Commissioned by the Habibie administration in July 1998 and chaired by Marzuki Darusman, uniting government officials, military prosecutors, and civil-society leaders such as Father Sandyawan Sumardi, Saparinah Sadli, and Abdul Hakim Garuda Nusantara. Its landmark October 1998 report verified systematic instigation, military dereliction of duty, and widespread gang rapes of ethnic Chinese-Indonesian women, recommending investigations into Lt. Gen. Prabowo Subianto and the May 14 Kostrad meeting.",
	"lib.lib_009.subtitle": "Joint Fact-Finding Team on the May 13-15, 1998 Riots",
	"lib.lib_009.term": "TGPF (Tim Gabungan Pencari Fakta Mei 1998)",
	"lib.lib_010.desc": "A constitutional advisory body under the unamended 1945 Constitution tasked with advising the President. Under Habibie, the DPA was chaired by South Sulawesi politician and businessman Arnold Achmad Baramuli, who used the post as a base for Habibie's informal re-election campaign team ([i]Tim Sukses[/i]) and became a central figure in the Bank Bali scandal.",
	"lib.lib_010.subtitle": "Supreme Advisory Council, Chaired in 1998-1999 by A.A. Baramuli",
	"lib.lib_010.term": "DPA (Dewan Pertimbangan Agung)",
	"lib.lib_011.desc": "Two independent oversight bodies created by 1999 reform statutes. KPKPN ([i]Komisi Pemeriksa Kekayaan Penyelenggara Negara[/i], created under Law No. 28/1999) was tasked with auditing the personal assets of cabinet ministers, legislators, and judges to root out illicit enrichment, serving as the direct forerunner of the modern KPK. KPPU ([i]Komisi Pengawas Persaingan Usaha[/i], created under Law No. 5/1999) was empowered to break up crony cartels and price-fixing syndicates.",
	"lib.lib_011.subtitle": "Post-1998 State Wealth Audit & Anti-Monopoly Commissions",
	"lib.lib_011.term": "KPKPN & KPPU",
	"lib.lib_012.desc": "Drafted on the eve of independence in 1945, reinstated by Sukarno's decree in July 1959, and treated as sacred and unamendable throughout Suharto's New Order. Containing only 37 brief articles, the unamended 1945 Constitution concentrated immense power in the Presidency while vesting formal sovereignty in the 700-seat MPR. In October 1999, the MPR began the historic process of passing the First Amendment to limit presidential power.",
	"lib.lib_012.subtitle": "The 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia",
	"lib.lib_012.term": "UUD 1945 (Undang-Undang Dasar 1945)",
	"lib.lib_013.desc": "Enacted on January 28, 1999 after intense negotiations in Senayan, this trio of statutes dismantled the New Order's rigged electoral system: Law No. 2/1999 ended the three-party cap and allowed dozens of new political parties to form freely; Law No. 3/1999 established proportional legislative elections overseen by an independent multiparty election commission (KPU); and Law No. 4/1999 restructured the DPR and MPR, slashing ABRI's unelected seats in half from 75 to 38.",
	"lib.lib_013.subtitle": "The 1999 Political, Electoral & Parliamentary Structure Laws",
	"lib.lib_013.term": "UU No. 2, 3 & 4 Tahun 1999 (Paket UU Politik 1999)",
	"lib.lib_014.desc": "Signed by President Habibie on March 5, 1999 to outlaw the state-backed cartels, price-fixing syndicates, and crony import monopolies (such as cloves, wheat flour, cement, and plywood) that had enriched the Suharto family and its [i]cukong[/i] partners. The law also created the independent Business Competition Supervisory Commission (KPPU).",
	"lib.lib_014.subtitle": "Law on the Prohibition of Monopolistic Practices and Unfair Business Competition",
	"lib.lib_014.term": "UU No. 5 Tahun 1999 (UU Anti-Monopoli)",
	"lib.lib_015.desc": "Enacted in April 1999 alongside the Anti-Monopoly Law as part of Indonesia's post-crisis economic reform package. It established statutory rights for Indonesian consumers against defective goods, fraudulent pricing, and coercive practices by state and private monopolies.",
	"lib.lib_015.subtitle": "The 1999 Consumer Protection Act",
	"lib.lib_015.term": "UU No. 8 Tahun 1999 (UU Perlindungan Konsumen)",
	"lib.lib_016.desc": "Enacted on October 26, 1998 following massive student protests, this law legalized public demonstrations, marches, and open-air rallies, replacing the New Order requirement for prior police permits with a three-day advance notification procedure, though student coalitions criticized its restrictions on night rallies and protests near presidential palaces.",
	"lib.lib_016.subtitle": "Law on Freedom of Expression in Public",
	"lib.lib_016.term": "UU No. 9 Tahun 1998 (Kemerdekaan Menyampaikan Pendapat)",
	"lib.lib_017.desc": "Signed in May 1999 and drafted by Ryaas Rasyid's 'Group of Seven' ([i]Tim Tujuh[/i]), these two statutes launched one of the most sweeping decentralizations in modern history. Law No. 22/1999 devolved administrative authority directly to regencies ([i]kabupaten[/i]) and cities, ending Jakarta's appointment of local officials, while Law No. 25/1999 guaranteed resource-rich provinces a direct share of forestry (80%), mining (80%), oil (15%), and natural gas (30%) revenues.",
	"lib.lib_017.subtitle": "The Regional Governance & Central-Regional Fiscal Balance Laws",
	"lib.lib_017.term": "UU No. 22 & 25 Tahun 1999 (UU Otonomi Daerah)",
	"lib.lib_018.desc": "Enacted on May 17, 1999, this landmark statute emancipated Bank Indonesia from the cabinet and the presidency, making the central bank an independent state institution with a single statutory mandate: maintaining the stability of the rupiah. Critically, it prohibited the President and Finance Ministry from ordering the central bank to print money to cover budget deficits or bail out crony banks as Suharto had done with BLBI.",
	"lib.lib_018.subtitle": "The Central Bank Independence Act",
	"lib.lib_018.term": "UU No. 23 Tahun 1999 (UU Bank Indonesia)",
	"lib.lib_019.desc": "Signed in May 1999, this statute formally repealed UU No. 11/PNPS/1963 (the Anti-Subversion Law), the notorious Cold War decree used for over three decades by the New Order to imprison political dissidents, student activists, labor organizers, and religious critics for up to life in prison or execution without proving a specific criminal act.",
	"lib.lib_019.subtitle": "Repeal of the 1963 Draconian Anti-Subversion Law",
	"lib.lib_019.term": "UU No. 26 Tahun 1999 (Pencabutan UU Anti-Subversi)",
	"lib.lib_020.desc": "Passed in May and August 1999 to fulfill the mandate of TAP MPR No. XI/MPR/1998. Law No. 28/1999 required all senior state officials, ministers, and judges to publicly declare their personal wealth before an audit commission (KPKPN), while Law No. 31/1999 overhauled criminal corruption statutes and mandated the creation of an independent Corruption Eradication Commission.",
	"lib.lib_020.subtitle": "Clean State Administration & Eradication of Corruption Laws",
	"lib.lib_020.term": "UU No. 28 & 31 Tahun 1999 (UU Anti-KKN & Tipikor)",
	"lib.lib_021.desc": "Enacted on August 31, 1999, amending Law No. 14/1970 on Judicial Power. For decades under the New Order, judges were civil servants under the Ministry of Justice, allowing the executive branch to punish independent judges with remote transfers. Law No. 35/1999 mandated the transfer of all organizational, administrative, and financial authority over Indonesia's courts to the independent Supreme Court (Mahkamah Agung).",
	"lib.lib_021.subtitle": "The 'One-Roof' (Satu Atap) Judicial Independence Amendment",
	"lib.lib_021.term": "UU No. 35 Tahun 1999 (Perubahan UU Kekuasaan Kehakiman)",
	"lib.lib_022.desc": "Enacted on September 23, 1999, codifying fundamental civil, political, women's, and children's rights into Indonesian statutory law. The act elevated Komnas HAM from a presidentially appointed body into an independent statutory commission armed with compulsory subpoena powers to investigate state and military human rights abuses.",
	"lib.lib_022.subtitle": "The Comprehensive Human Rights Act",
	"lib.lib_022.term": "UU No. 39 Tahun 1999 (UU Hak Asasi Manusia)",
	"lib.lib_023.desc": "Signed by President Habibie on September 23, 1999, this landmark law permanently outlawed press censorship, licensing requirements (SIUPP), and arbitrary government bans on newspapers, magazines, and broadcast outlets. It imposed criminal prison sentences and fines on anyone who unlawfully obstructs journalistic work and established an independent Press Council ([i]Dewan Pers[/i]).",
	"lib.lib_023.subtitle": "The Freedom of the Press Act",
	"lib.lib_023.term": "UU No. 40 Tahun 1999 (UU Pers)",
	"lib.lib_024.desc": "Enacted in September 1999 to reform the corrupt New Order forest concession system (HPH) that had allowed timber barons like Bob Hasan and military foundations to clear-cut millions of hectares of rainforest in Kalimantan and Sumatra whilerecognizing customary forest categories ([i]hutan adat[/i]) and tighter conservation rules.",
	"lib.lib_024.subtitle": "The 1999 Forestry Reform Law",
	"lib.lib_024.term": "UU No. 41 Tahun 1999 (UU Kehutanan)",
	"lib.lib_025.desc": "Signed by President Habibie on October 4, 1999 in a bid to calm separatist anger in Aceh prior to the MPR General Session. The statute formally guaranteed Aceh special autonomy over religious life (including the implementation of Islamic law elements), customary law ([i]adat[/i]), education, and the advisory role of religious scholars ([i]ulama[/i]).",
	"lib.lib_025.subtitle": "Law on the Special Status of the Province of Aceh",
	"lib.lib_025.term": "UU No. 44 Tahun 1999 (Keistimewaan Aceh)",
	"lib.lib_026.desc": "Signed in early October 1999. Law No. 45/1999 partitioned Irian Jaya (Papua) into West, Central, and East Irian Jaya provinces, sparking fierce protests in Jayapura from Papuan leaders who saw it as a divide-and-rule tactic, while Law No. 46/1999 separated North Maluku into its own province amid raging communal conflict in the Moluccas.",
	"lib.lib_026.subtitle": "Laws Dividing Irian Jaya into Three Provinces & Creating North Maluku",
	"lib.lib_026.term": "UU No. 45 & 46 Tahun 1999 (Pemekaran Irian Jaya & Maluku Utara)",
	"lib.lib_027.desc": "Signed on June 5, 1998, just two weeks into Habibie's presidency. By ratifying International Labour Organization Convention No. 87, the decree ended the New Order's thirty-year ban on independent labor unions, legalizing Muchtar Pakpahan's SBSI and allowing factory workers, journalists, and teachers to organize outside the state-controlled SPSI.",
	"lib.lib_027.subtitle": "Presidential Decree Ratifying ILO Freedom of Association",
	"lib.lib_027.term": "Keppres No. 83 Tahun 1998 (Ratifikasi Konvensi ILO No. 87)",
	"lib.lib_028.desc": "Rushed through the outgoing DPR in September 1999 at the behest of General Wiranto and the Defense Ministry, RUU PKB granted the President and military commanders sweeping emergency powers to ban protests, censor communications, search homes without warrants, and impose military rule during 'social unrest.' Massive student demonstrations against its passage triggered the deadly Semanggi II Tragedy (September 24, 1999), forcing President Habibie to delay signing the bill indefinitely.",
	"lib.lib_028.subtitle": "The Controversial 1999 State of Danger / Martial Law Bill",
	"lib.lib_028.term": "RUU PKB (Rancangan Undang-Undang Penanggulangan Keadaan Bahaya)",
	"lib.lib_029.desc": "Enacted in January 1999, these regulations prohibited Indonesia's four million civil servants (PNS) from joining or campaigning for political parties without taking leave from their state posts, shattering thirty years of compulsory KORPRI monoloyalty to Golkar and leveling the electoral playing field for the June 1999 ballot.",
	"lib.lib_029.subtitle": "Government Regulations on Civil Service Political Neutrality",
	"lib.lib_029.term": "PP No. 5 & PP No. 12 Tahun 1999 (Netralitas PNS)",
	"lib.lib_030.desc": "Suharto's Inpres No. 14/1967 banned public expressions of Chinese religion, festivals, and script across Indonesia for over thirty years. Following the May 1998 anti-Chinese pogroms, President Habibie issued Inpres No. 26/1998 on September 16, 1998, ordering all government ministries, state banks, and universities to abolish the official use of the terms [i]pribumi[/i] (indigenous) and [i]non-pribumi[/i] and to end discriminatory administrative rules.",
	"lib.lib_030.subtitle": "Decrees on Ethnic Chinese-Indonesian Rights & Cultural Restrictions",
	"lib.lib_030.term": "Inpres No. 26 Tahun 1998 & Inpres No. 14 Tahun 1967",
	"lib.lib_031.desc": "Passed during the turbulent November 1998 MPR Special Session ([i]Sidang Istimewa[/i]): TAP MPR No. XI/MPR/1998 mandated a clean state free of KKN and explicitly mentioned former President Suharto by name in Article 4 as a target of corruption investigations; TAP MPR No. XIII/MPR/1998 ended lifelong presidencies by limiting the President and Vice President of Indonesia to a maximum of two five-year terms.",
	"lib.lib_031.subtitle": "MPR Special Session Decrees on KKN, Suharto & Presidential Term Limits",
	"lib.lib_031.term": "TAP MPR No. XI/MPR/1998 & TAP MPR No. XIII/MPR/1998",
	"lib.lib_032.desc": "Enacted on the night of October 19-20, 1999 during the MPR General Session: TAP MPR No. III/MPR/1999 formally recorded the Assembly's secret-ballot vote rejecting President B.J. Habibie's Accountability Speech; TAP MPR No. V/MPR/1999 formally revoked the 1978 annexation decree (TAP MPR No. VI/MPR/1978) and recognized the outcome of the August 30 East Timor independence ballot.",
	"lib.lib_032.subtitle": "The October 1999 General Session Decrees on Habibie & East Timor",
	"lib.lib_032.term": "TAP MPR No. III/MPR/1999 & TAP MPR No. V/MPR/1999",
	"lib.lib_033.desc": "The legal foundation of late-Suharto authoritarianism (Laws No. 1, 2, 3, 5, and 8 of 1985). Together, they locked in the three-party system (Golkar, PPP, PDI), required every political party and civic association ([i]Ormas[/i]) to adopt Pancasila as its sole ideological foundation ([i]Asas Tunggal[/i]), packed the MPR with presidentially appointed delegates, and required a 90% supermajority plus a national referendum to ever amend the 1945 Constitution.",
	"lib.lib_033.subtitle": "The Five 1985 New Order Political Control Statutes",
	"lib.lib_033.term": "Paket 5 UU Politik 1985",
	"lib.lib_034.desc": "Created in 1964 and transformed by Suharto into the ruling electoral machine of the New Order, winning between 62% and 74% in six consecutive stage-managed elections (1971-1997). Reorganized into Partai Golkar under chairman Akbar Tandjung at the July 1998 Extraordinary Congress, Golkar finished second in the June 1999 election with 22.4% of the vote (120 DPR seats), but fractured bitterly between Habibie's outer-island/ICMI loyalists ('White Golkar' / [i]Irama Tersedak[/i]) and Akbar Tandjung's pragmatic Slipi party apparatus ('Black Golkar').",
	"lib.lib_034.subtitle": "Party of Functional Groups: 'The Banyan Tree' (Pohon Beringin)",
	"lib.lib_034.term": "Golkar (Golongan Karya)",
	"lib.lib_035.desc": "Founded by Megawati Sukarnoputri (daughter of founding President Sukarno) after the Suharto regime forcibly ousted her from the leadership of the state-sanctioned PDI in the bloody July 27, 1996 crackdown ([i]Kudatuli[/i]). Anchored in secular Pancasila nationalism and the urban and rural poor ([i]wong cilik[/i]), PDI-P won a commanding plurality in the June 1999 election with 33.7% of the vote and 153 seats in the MPR.",
	"lib.lib_035.subtitle": "Indonesian Democratic Party of Struggle: 'The White-Muzzled Bull'",
	"lib.lib_035.term": "PDI-P (Partai Demokrasi Indonesia Perjuangan)",
	"lib.lib_036.desc": "Founded in July 1998 by Abdurrahman Wahid (Gus Dur) and the clerics ([i]kyai[/i]) of Nahdlatul Ulama (NU), Indonesia's largest traditionalist Muslim organization. PKB combined pluralist, inclusive nationalist politics with rural Javanese Islamic boarding-school ([i]pesantren[/i]) networks, winning 12.6% of the national vote and 51 seats in the 1999 MPR.",
	"lib.lib_036.subtitle": "National Awakening Party",
	"lib.lib_036.term": "PKB (Partai Kebangkitan Bangsa)",
	"lib.lib_037.desc": "Formed in 1973 when the New Order forcibly merged four Islamic parties (NU, Parmusi, PSII, and Perti) into a single supervised Islamic opposition party. Chaired in 1999 by Hamzah Haz, PPP reclaimed the [i]Ka'bah[/i] as its symbol and Islam as its party foundation after Suharto's fall, winning 58 seats in the 1999 MPR.",
	"lib.lib_037.subtitle": "United Development Party: 'The Ka'bah Party'",
	"lib.lib_037.term": "PPP (Partai Persatuan Pembangunan)",
	"lib.lib_038.desc": "Founded in August 1998 by Dr. Amien Rais, former chairman of the modernist Islamic organization Muhammadiyah and the most outspoken national leader of the May 1998 student uprising. Though running on an inclusive, pluralist reform platform, PAN drew heavily on urban modernist Muslim voters, winning 34 seats in the DPR and serving as the pivot of the 1999 MPR session after Amien Rais was elected MPR Speaker.",
	"lib.lib_038.subtitle": "National Mandate Party",
	"lib.lib_038.term": "PAN (Partai Amanat Nasional)",
	"lib.lib_039.desc": "A political coalition engineered in mid-1999 by PAN leader Amien Rais uniting PAN, Hamzah Haz's PPP, Gus Dur's PKB, Yusril Ihza Mahendra's Crescent Star Party (PBB), and Nurmahmudi Ismail's Justice Party (PK). Designed to break the polarized deadlock between B.J. Habibie and Megawati Sukarnoputri, the Central Axis became the decisive kingmaker of the October 1999 General Session by floating Gus Dur as a compromise presidential candidate.",
	"lib.lib_039.subtitle": "The 1999 Modernist & Traditionalist Islamic Kingmaker Coalition",
	"lib.lib_039.term": "Poros Tengah (The Central Axis)",
	"lib.lib_040.desc": "Because PAN won 34 seats and the campus-based Justice Party (Partai Keadilan) won 7 seats in the June 1999 election, the two parties joined forces on the floor of the DPR and MPR to form the 41-seat Fraksi Reformasi, chaired by Hatta Rajasa and AM Fatwa, giving Amien Rais a disciplined parliamentary caucus to spearhead the Central Axis.",
	"lib.lib_040.subtitle": "The 41-Seat Reformist Parliamentary Alliance in the 1999 DPR/MPR",
	"lib.lib_040.term": "Fraksi Reformasi (PAN & Partai Keadilan Caucus)",
	"lib.lib_041.desc": "Two influential modernist Islamic parties formed in 1998 that sat inside the minor-party bloc and joined the [i]Poros Tengah[/i]. Partai Bulan Bintang (PBB) (13 seats), led by constitutional law professor Yusril Ihza Mahendra and hardline preacher Ahmad Sumargono, saw itself as the heir to the 1950s Masyumi party and was initially sympathetic to Habibie. Partai Keadilan (PK) (7 seats, later PKS), led by Nurmahmudi Ismail and Hidayat Nur Wahid, grew out of the university campus mosque movement ([i]Tarbiyah[/i] / KAMMI).",
	"lib.lib_041.subtitle": "The Modernist Islamic & Campus Da'wah Parties",
	"lib.lib_041.term": "PBB (Partai Bulan Bintang) & Partai Keadilan (PK)",
	"lib.lib_042.desc": "Founded in late 1998 by former Armed Forces Commander and Defense Minister Gen. (Ret.) Edi Sudradjat and Gen. (Ret.) Try Sutrisno after they lost the July 1998 Golkar chairmanship battle to Akbar Tandjung and Habibie's ICMI camp. Fiercely secular-nationalist and hostile to political Islam and Habibie, PKP anchored the F-KKI ([i]Fraksi Kesatuan Kebangsaan Indonesia[/i]) minor-party coalition alongside Christian and pluralist parties like Manasse Malo's PDKB.",
	"lib.lib_042.subtitle": "Secular-Nationalist Retired Generals' Breakaway Party",
	"lib.lib_042.term": "PKP (Partai Keadilan dan Persatuan) & F-KKI",
	"lib.lib_043.desc": "After the Suharto regime engineered the ouster of Megawati Sukarnoputri at the 1996 Medan Congress and handed the official PDI headquarters to Suryadi (and later Budi Hardjono), the state-backed PDI retained the party's old name for the June 1999 election. Voters abandoned it almost completely: the official PDI won just 2 seats nationally while Megawati's PDI-P won 153.",
	"lib.lib_043.subtitle": "The State-Backed PDI Shell Crushed in the June 1999 Ballot",
	"lib.lib_043.term": "PDI (Suryadi / Budi Hardjono Rump Party)",
	"lib.lib_044.desc": "Founded in 1996 by young left-wing student and labor activists led by Budiman Sudjatmiko and poet Wiji Thukul. Blamed by Suharto for the July 27, 1996 Jakarta riots, its leaders were hunted down, abducted by Kopassus Team Mawar, or imprisoned in Cipinang. Allowed to contest the June 1999 election after Habibie lifted the ban on the party, PRD remained a vocal extraparliamentary force demanding the immediate abolition of ABRI's political role.",
	"lib.lib_044.subtitle": "People's Democratic Party: The Leftist Student & Labor Vanguard",
	"lib.lib_044.term": "PRD (Partai Rakyat Demokratik)",
	"lib.lib_045.desc": "The 200 non-DPR civilian delegates inside the 700-seat MPR. Utusan Daerah (135 seats, 5 from each of the 27 provinces) were elected by provincial parliaments (DPRD I) and included governors, regional party bosses, and local notables. Utusan Golongan (65 seats) were selected from religious councils (MUI, PGI, KWI), labor unions, women's groups, cooperative movements, and universities. Lacking rigid party whips, both blocs were prime targets for intense lobbying during October 1999.",
	"lib.lib_045.subtitle": "The 135 Regional Delegates & 65 Functional Group Delegates",
	"lib.lib_045.term": "Utusan Daerah & Utusan Golongan",
	"lib.lib_046.desc": "Indonesia's military establishment, commanded throughout 1998-1999 by General Wiranto (who served concurrently as Commander of the Armed Forces and Minister of Defense and Security). Prior to April 1, 1999, ABRI encompassed the Army, Navy, Air Force, and the National Police (Polri). Even after its reserved parliamentary bloc was halved from 75 to 38 unelected seats for the 1999 election, ABRI voted with iron command discipline.",
	"lib.lib_046.subtitle": "Armed Forces of the Republic of Indonesia (Renamed TNI in April 1999)",
	"lib.lib_046.term": "ABRI / TNI (Angkatan Bersenjata Republik Indonesia)",
	"lib.lib_047.desc": "Originated by Gen. A.H. Nasution in 1958 ('the Middle Way') and codified into law under Suharto, [i]Dwifungsi[/i] held that the Indonesian military was not merely a defense force, but also a permanent sociopolitical force entitled to hold cabinet ministries, governorships, civil service posts, state enterprise boards, and unelected seats in the DPR and MPR. Abolishing [i]Dwifungsi ABRI[/i] was one of the core demands of the 1998-1999 student movement.",
	"lib.lib_047.subtitle": "The Military's Sociopolitical & Security Mandate",
	"lib.lib_047.term": "Dwifungsi ABRI (Dual Function Doctrine)",
	"lib.lib_048.desc": "Unveiled in late 1998 by ABRI Chief of Sociopolitical Affairs Lt. Gen. Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono (SBY) and Gen. Agus Widjojo, the 'New Paradigm' sought to preserve the military's institutional survival by voluntarily retreating from day-to-day front-line politics, ending active-duty secondments ([i]kekaryaan[/i]) to the civilian bureaucracy, severing formal ties with Golkar, and separating the National Police (Polri) from the military command structure on April 1, 1999.",
	"lib.lib_048.subtitle": "The 1998 Military Reform Doctrine & Separation of the National Police",
	"lib.lib_048.term": "Paradigma Baru ABRI & Pemisahan Polri",
	"lib.lib_049.desc": "Journalistic shorthand for the rivalry inside the 1990s military brass. ABRI Merah-Putih ('Red-and-White ABRI') referred to secular-nationalist officers shaped by longtime intelligence chief Gen. Benny Moerdani (such as Edi Sudradjat, Try Sutrisno, and Hendropriyono) who distrusted political Islam and Habibie's ICMI. ABRI Hijau ('Green ABRI') referred to officers promoted by Suharto in the 1990s who cultivated close ties with ICMI and Muslim organizations (such as Gen. Feisal Tanjung, Gen. R. Hartono, Lt. Gen. Prabowo Subianto, and Maj. Gen. Kivlan Zen).",
	"lib.lib_049.subtitle": "The 1990s Factional Divide Inside the Indonesian Officer Corps",
	"lib.lib_049.term": "ABRI Hijau vs. ABRI Merah-Putih",
	"lib.lib_050.desc": "The sprawling military headquarters complex in East Jakarta housing the Armed Forces High Command (Mabes ABRI/TNI). In Indonesian political shorthand, 'Cilangkap' refers to the military brass and General Wiranto's inner circle, just as 'Bina Graha' or 'Merdeka Palace' refers to the Presidency and 'Slipi' refers to Golkar's central party headquarters.",
	"lib.lib_050.subtitle": "Armed Forces Headquarters in East Jakarta",
	"lib.lib_050.term": "Cilangkap",
	"lib.lib_051.desc": "The backbone of military control over Indonesian society. The Army's Territorial Command structure mirrors every single tier of the civilian government: Kodam (Regional Military Commands covering major island groups and provinces), Korem (Sub-regional commands), Kodim (District commands matching regencies), Koramil (Sub-district commands), and Babinsa (village-level non-commissioned officers). Through this network, the Army monitored local politics, labor strikes, and elections in every village and generated vast off-budget revenue through military business foundations ([i]yayasan[/i]).",
	"lib.lib_051.subtitle": "The Army's Parallel Territorial Command Hierarchy",
	"lib.lib_051.term": "Kodam, Korem, Kodim, Koramil & Babinsa",
	"lib.lib_052.desc": "Indonesia's two premier central combat formations. Kostrad is the 35,000-troop rapid-deployment strategic reserve command (which Suharto's son-in-law Lt. Gen. Prabowo Subianto commanded until Habibie and Wiranto relieved him on May 22, 1998). Kopassus is the elite red-beret special forces command, whose covert Group IV unit, codenamed Tim Mawar ('Rose Team'), abducted and tortured more than twenty pro-democracy activists in 1997-1998, thirteen of whom were forcibly disappeared.",
	"lib.lib_052.subtitle": "Army Strategic Reserve, Special Forces & the Covert Abduction Squad",
	"lib.lib_052.term": "Kostrad, Kopassus & Tim Mawar",
	"lib.lib_053.desc": "Convened by General Wiranto in August 1998 and chaired by Army Chief of Staff Gen. Subagyo H.S. (including Lt. Gen. Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono and Lt. Gen. Agum Gumelar) to investigate the abduction of pro-democracy activists by Kopassus troops. Its ruling resulted in the administrative discharge of Lt. Gen. Prabowo Subianto from military service, though he was never brought before a court-martial.",
	"lib.lib_053.subtitle": "The August 1998 Military Officers' Honor Council",
	"lib.lib_053.term": "DKP (Dewan Kehormatan Perwira)",
	"lib.lib_054.desc": "BAKIN ([i]Badan Koordinasi Intelijen Negara[/i]), headed under Habibie by Lt. Gen. (Ret.) Z.A. Maulani, served as the civilian-executive intelligence agency and was frequently accused of wiretapping opposition leaders. BIA (reorganized as BAIS in 1999) was the military's strategic intelligence agency under Cilangkap, deeply involved in covert operations in East Timor. Bakorstanas (the successor to the feared [i]Kopkamtib[/i]) was the military internal-security body used to monitor dissidents and labor strikes.",
	"lib.lib_054.subtitle": "New Order Intelligence & Internal Security Apparatus",
	"lib.lib_054.term": "BAKIN, BIA (BAIS) & Bakorstanas",
	"lib.lib_055.desc": "Pam Swakarsa ('Voluntary Security Guards') were civilian youth and religious militias armed with sharpened bamboo spears and trucked into Jakarta by military intelligence during the November 1998 MPR Special Session to confront student demonstrators, triggering bloody clashes around Semanggi. Kamra ([i]Keamanan Rakyat[/i], or 'People's Security') was a formal 40,000-man auxiliary police force recruited by the Defense Ministry in 1999 under the guise of unemployment relief.",
	"lib.lib_055.subtitle": "Civilian Auxiliary Militia Forces Mobilized in 1998-1999",
	"lib.lib_055.term": "Kamra & Pam Swakarsa",
	"lib.lib_056.desc": "The counter-insurgency regime imposed on Aceh by Jakarta for nearly a decade to crush the Free Aceh Movement (GAM). Thousands of Acehnese civilians were tortured, disappeared, or executed at sites like Rumoh Geudong. Although General Wiranto lifted DOM in August 1998 and President Habibie traveled to Banda Aceh in March 1999 to publicly apologize, renewed military massacres at Simpang KKA (May 1999) and Beutong Ateuh (July 1999) shattered public trust and fueled a massive movement for an Acehnese independence referendum.",
	"lib.lib_056.subtitle": "Military Operations Area Designation in Aceh (1989-1998)",
	"lib.lib_056.term": "DOM (Daerah Operasi Militer)",
	"lib.lib_057.desc": "Paramilitary groups organized, armed, and funded by local ABRI territorial and special-forces officers in East Timor ahead of the August 30, 1999 UN referendum. Aitarak ('Thorn'), led by Eurico Guterres in Dili, and Besi Merah Putih ('Red and White Iron') in Liquica carried out church massacres, political assassinations, and the scorched-earth destruction of Dili after 78.5% of East Timorese voted for independence.",
	"lib.lib_057.subtitle": "Military-Backed Pro-Integration Militias in East Timor (1999)",
	"lib.lib_057.term": "Aitarak & Besi Merah Putih",
	"lib.lib_058.desc": "Triggered after the floating of the Thai baht in July 1997, [i]Krismon[/i] devastated Indonesia more severely than any other country in Asia. Crushed by $80 billion in unhedged private corporate dollar debt and rampant crony banking practices, the rupiah plunged from Rp 2,430 per US dollar in June 1997 to nearly Rp 16,800 in early 1998. GDP contracted by 13.1% in 1998, food inflation exceeded 80%, and tens of millions fell into poverty, shattering the New Order's economic legitimacy.",
	"lib.lib_058.subtitle": "The 1997-1998 Asian Financial Crisis in Indonesia",
	"lib.lib_058.term": "Krismon (Krisis Moneter)",
	"lib.lib_059.desc": "Over Rp 144.5 trillion in emergency central-bank liquidity injected into 48 failing private commercial banks during the 1997-1998 bank runs. Rather than using the funds to pay depositors, many politically connected bank owners diverted the money into foreign currency speculation, affiliated conglomerate companies, and offshore accounts in Singapore, leaving Indonesian taxpayers with a massive sovereign debt burden.",
	"lib.lib_059.subtitle": "Bank Indonesia Emergency Liquidity Assistance",
	"lib.lib_059.term": "BLBI (Bantuan Likuiditas Bank Indonesia)",
	"lib.lib_060.desc": "Established in January 1998 under the IMF program (headed in 1998-1999 by Glenn Yusuf), IBRA (BPPN) took over collapsed banks and seized corporate assets from defaulting BLBI debtors. Under controversial MSAA (Master Settlement and Acquisition Agreements) negotiated in late 1998, major conglomerate owners (such as Anthony Salim of BCA/Salim Group and Sjamsul Nursalim of BDNI) pledged industrial and plantation assets to IBRA in exchange for immunity from criminal prosecution ('Release and Discharge'), though subsequent audits found the surrendered assets were worth only a fraction of their claimed value.",
	"lib.lib_060.subtitle": "Indonesian Bank Restructuring Agency & Tycoon Debt Settlements",
	"lib.lib_060.term": "IBRA / BPPN & MSAA Agreements",
	"lib.lib_061.desc": "Uncovered in July and August 1999, the scandal centered on a suspicious Rp 546 billion ($70 million) 'commission' paid by state-supervised Bank Bali (out of Rp 904 billion in IBRA interbank guarantees) to PT Era Giat Prima (EGP), a firm run by Golkar deputy treasurer Setya Novanto and Djoko S. Tjandra. Audits by PricewaterhouseCoopers (PwC) and leaked testimony revealed that the money was intended to bankroll Habibie's re-election campaign in the October MPR session via an informal Palace team known as [i]Tim Sukses[/i], prompting the IMF and World Bank to freeze aid disbursements in September 1999.",
	"lib.lib_061.subtitle": "The Rp 546 Billion Campaign Slush-Fund Scandal of Mid-1999",
	"lib.lib_061.term": "Bank Bali Scandal ('Baligate')",
	"lib.lib_062.desc": "Named after former President Suharto's residence at No. 8 Jalan Cendana in Menteng. Suharto's six children built sprawling corporate empires backed by state licenses and state bank loans: middle son Bambang Trihatmodjo headed the Bimantara Group (television RCTI, satellites, shipping, and petrochemicals); eldest daughter Tutut (Siti Hardiyanti Rukmana) headed Citra Lamtoro Gung Persada (Jakarta toll roads, television TPI, and infrastructure); and youngest son Tommy (Hutomo Mandala Putra) headed the Humpuss Group (Pertamina oil transport, Sempati Air, cloves, and the Timor car).",
	"lib.lib_062.subtitle": "Bimantara, Citra Lamtoro Gung & Humpuss",
	"lib.lib_062.term": "Jalan Cendana & The Suharto Conglomerates",
	"lib.lib_063.desc": "Under Presidential Decrees issued by Suharto, state-owned banks, civil servants, and private corporations were required to tithe a percentage of their profits and salaries into tax-exempt foundations personally chaired by Suharto, including Yayasan Supersemar, Dakab (which bankrolled Golkar campaigns), Dharmais, and Amalbhakti Muslim Pancasila. Rather than charity, trillions of rupiah were funneled as low-interest loans into companies owned by Suharto's children and cronies like Bob Hasan.",
	"lib.lib_063.subtitle": "Suharto's Tax-Free Multi-Billion-Dollar 'Charitable' Foundations",
	"lib.lib_063.term": "Yayasan Supersemar, Dakab, Dharmais & Amalbhakti",
	"lib.lib_064.desc": "The BPPC (Clove Marketing and Buffer Stock Agency) forced Indonesia's clove farmers to sell their crops to Tommy Suharto's syndicate at half price while charging cigarette makers double. In 1996, Inpres No. 2/1996 granted Tommy's PT Timor Putra Nasional exclusive exemption from luxury and import tariffs to import rebadged Kia sedans as the 'National Car.' Meanwhile, the off-budget Reforestation Fund ([i]Dana Reboisasi[/i]) was tapped by presidential decrees to finance both crony pulp mills and a Rp 400 billion loan to Habibie's IPTN aircraft factory.",
	"lib.lib_064.subtitle": "Flagship Monopolies & Off-Budget Funds of the Late New Order",
	"lib.lib_064.term": "BPPC, Mobil Nasional 'Timor' & Dana Reboisasi",
	"lib.lib_065.desc": "The rallying cry of the 1998 [i]Reformasi[/i] movement, encapsulating the systemic entanglement of state power, crony conglomerates, and the Suharto family. Eradicating KKN and bringing Suharto and his family to trial was the primary benchmark by which students and the public judged the credibility of Habibie's government.",
	"lib.lib_065.subtitle": "Corruption, Collusion, and Nepotism",
	"lib.lib_065.term": "KKN (Korupsi, Kolusi, dan Nepotisme)",
	"lib.lib_066.desc": "For twenty years as Minister of Research and Technology (1978-1998), Habibie led the Agency for the Assessment and Application of Technology (BPPT) and the Strategic Industries Management Agency (BPIS), overseeing ten state-owned high-tech enterprises. Its crown jewel was IPTN ([i]Industri Pesawat Terbang Nusantara[/i]) in Bandung, which designed the fly-by-wire N-250 Gatotkaca turboprop airliner. In 1998, the IMF forced Jakarta to cut off state budget subsidies to IPTN as a condition of its bailout.",
	"lib.lib_066.subtitle": "Habibie's Strategic Hi-Tech Industrial Complex",
	"lib.lib_066.term": "IPTN, BPPT & BPIS",
	"lib.lib_067.desc": "Bulog ([i]Badan Urusan Logistik[/i]) is the state commodity agency responsible for stabilizing the price and supply of Indonesia's nine basic necessities (Sembako: rice, sugar, cooking oil, meat and fish, eggs, milk, corn, kerosene, and salt). Under the New Order, Bulog's import monopolies were a major source of off-budget patronage for politically connected distributors; under IMF pressure in 1998-1999, many of its non-rice monopolies were dismantled.",
	"lib.lib_067.subtitle": "The State Logistics Agency & Nine Basic Necessities",
	"lib.lib_067.term": "Bulog & Sembako",
	"lib.lib_068.desc": "To cushion the devastating social impact of [i]Krismon[/i], the Habibie government launched the World Bank-backed JPS ([i]Jaringan Pengaman Sosial[/i] / Social Safety Net) providing subsidized rice (OPK) and emergency public-works employment. Simultaneously, Cooperatives Minister Adi Sasono championed Ekonomi Kerakyatan ('People's Economy'), channeling trillions of rupiah in subsidized state credit to thousands of rural cooperatives and small pribumi businesses, a program praised by populists but attacked by critics as a vote-buying machine for October 1999.",
	"lib.lib_068.subtitle": "Social Safety Net & Adi Sasono's 'People's Economy' Program",
	"lib.lib_068.term": "JPS & Ekonomi Kerakyatan",
	"lib.lib_069.desc": "A generation of US-educated Indonesian economists from the University of Indonesia's Faculty of Economics (originally led by Widjojo Nitisastro and Ali Wardhana) who championed balanced budgets, open capital accounts, and export-oriented manufacturing. Throughout the 1980s and 1990s, the technocrats clashed constantly with B.J. Habibie's 'technologues' over state spending on tariff-protected heavy industry and aircraft manufacturing.",
	"lib.lib_069.subtitle": "Orthodox Neoclassical Economists at the Ministry of Finance & Bappenas",
	"lib.lib_069.term": "Berkeley Mafia (The UI Technocrats)",
	"lib.lib_070.desc": "Founded in Malang in December 1990 with Suharto's blessing and chaired by B.J. Habibie. ICMI brought modernist Muslim academics, engineers, bureaucrats, and activists, including Adi Sasono, Jimly Asshiddiqie, Dewi Fortuna Anwar, and early on Amien Rais, into the corridors of power, founding the daily newspaper [i]Republika[/i] and serving as Habibie's core political and intellectual base in 1998-1999.",
	"lib.lib_070.subtitle": "Association of Indonesian Muslim Intellectuals",
	"lib.lib_070.term": "ICMI (Ikatan Cendekiawan Muslim se-Indonesia)",
	"lib.lib_071.desc": "Nahdlatul Ulama (NU), founded in 1926 and led in 1999 by Abdurrahman Wahid (Gus Dur), represents over 35 to 40 million traditionalist Muslims anchored in rural Islamic boarding schools ([i]pesantren[/i]), particularly in East and Central Java, and forms the backbone of PKB. Muhammadiyah, founded in 1912 and led until 1998 by Amien Rais, represents 25 to 30 million modernist Muslims with a vast network of urban universities, schools, and hospitals, forming the core base of PAN.",
	"lib.lib_071.subtitle": "Indonesia's Two Largest Islamic Mass Organizations",
	"lib.lib_071.term": "Nahdlatul Ulama (NU) & Muhammadiyah",
	"lib.lib_072.desc": "The student movements that spearheaded the overthrow of Suharto in May 1998 and mobilized tens of thousands of protesters during Habibie's presidency. Radical cross-campus street coalitions such as Forkot (City Forum), Famred, and FKSMJ demanded an immediate 'Transitional Presidium' and the abolition of [i]Dwifungsi ABRI[/i]; KAMMI mobilized campus mosque activists; while the established Cipayung Group associations, led by HMI (Muslim Students' Association, Akbar Tandjung's political alma mater), PMII, and GMNI, bridged street activism and parliamentary party politics.",
	"lib.lib_072.subtitle": "The 1998-1999 University Student Action Coalitions & Associations",
	"lib.lib_072.term": "Forkot, Famred, KAMMI & Kelompok Cipayung",
	"lib.lib_073.desc": "The institutional conscience of the [i]Reformasi[/i] era. The Indonesian Legal Aid Foundation (YLBHI / LBH) defended political prisoners throughout the New Order and incubated KontraS (Commission for the Disappeared and Victims of Violence), founded in March 1998 by lawyer Munir Said Thalib. TRuK (Volunteer Team for Humanity), led by Father Sandyawan Sumardi, documented the May 1998 burnings and mass gang rapes of Chinese-Indonesian women, leading to the creation of Komnas Perempuan (National Commission on Violence Against Women) in October 1998.",
	"lib.lib_073.subtitle": "Legal Aid, Human Rights & Survivors' Advocacy Organizations",
	"lib.lib_073.term": "YLBHI (LBH), KontraS, TRuK & Komnas Perempuan",
	"lib.lib_074.desc": "Under the New Order, all journalists were forced into the state-supervised PWI and all factory workers into the state-controlled union SPSI. After the 1994 banning of [i]Tempo[/i], dissident reporters led by Goenawan Mohamad and Ahmad Taufik founded the underground Alliance of Independent Journalists (AJI). Similarly, labor leader Muchtar Pakpahan founded the independent union SBSI ([i]Serikat Buruh Sejahtera Indonesia[/i]). Both were legalized by the Habibie administration in mid-1998.",
	"lib.lib_074.subtitle": "Independent Journalists' & Trade Union Movements",
	"lib.lib_074.term": "AJI (Aliansi Jurnalis Independen) & SPSI / SBSI",
	"lib.lib_075.desc": "A historic meeting held on November 10, 1998 at Gus Dur's residence in Ciganjur, South Jakarta, bringing together the four most prominent figures outside Habibie's administration: Abdurrahman Wahid (Gus Dur), Megawati Sukarnoputri, Amien Rais, and the revered Sultan of Yogyakarta, Sri Sultan Hamengkubuwono X. Their joint declaration committed the opposition to peaceful constitutional reform, fair elections in 1999, and a phased six-year end to [i]Dwifungsi ABRI[/i], preventing a revolutionary street overthrow during the November 1998 MPR Special Session.",
	"lib.lib_075.subtitle": "The November 10, 1998 Opposition Reform Summit",
	"lib.lib_075.term": "Deklarasi Ciganjur (The Ciganjur Four)",
	"lib.lib_076.desc": "Following the May 5, 1999 Tripartite Agreement signed by Indonesia, Portugal, and the UN, the United Nations Mission in East Timor (UNAMET), led by Ian Martin, organized the August 30, 1999 popular consultation. The pro-independence movement was led politically by the CNRT under imprisoned leader Xanana Gusmao and armed guerrilla wing Falintil, while pro-Indonesia militias were backed by local ABRI units. After 78.5% voted for independence and militias torched Dili, the Australian-led multinational force INTERFET deployed in September 1999.",
	"lib.lib_076.subtitle": "Key Forces in the 1999 East Timor Independence Consultation",
	"lib.lib_076.term": "UNAMET, CNRT, Falintil & INTERFET",
	"lib.lib_077.desc": "GAM (Gerakan Aceh Merdeka / Free Aceh Movement), founded by Hasan di Tiro in 1976, waged an armed insurgency in Aceh, while the student-led coalition SIRA (Aceh Referendum Information Center), inspired by East Timor's ballot, mobilized hundreds of thousands of Acehnese demanding a peaceful independence referendum. In Irian Jaya (Papua), the OPM (Free Papua Movement) and the Team of 100 civil-society delegation (led by Tom Beanal and Theys Eluay, who stunned President Habibie in a face-to-face meeting at Istana Negara on February 26, 1999) demanded immediate independence from Indonesia.",
	"lib.lib_077.subtitle": "Independence & Referendum Movements in Aceh and Irian Jaya (Papua)",
	"lib.lib_077.term": "GAM, SIRA & OPM (Tim 100)",
	"lib.lib_078.desc": "Peristiwa Kudatuli (July 27, 1996) was the bloody military-backed raid on Megawati Sukarnoputri's PDI headquarters on Jalan Diponegoro that sparked the worst Jakarta riots in twenty years. Two years later, between August and October 1998, the Banyuwangi 'Ninja' Killings saw over 140 traditional healers ([i]dukun santet[/i]) and Nahdlatul Ulama religious teachers ([i]kyai[/i]) assassinated across East Java by black-clad hitmen, an episode Gus Dur accused regime hardliners of orchestrating to terrorize his rural NU base.",
	"lib.lib_078.subtitle": "Political Violence & Covert Provocations of the Late Transition",
	"lib.lib_078.term": "Peristiwa Kudatuli (27 Juli 1996) & Tragedi Banyuwangi (1998)",
	"lib.lib_079.desc": "A constitutional report delivered by the incumbent President to the MPR at the end of a term, accounting for state policy, security, and finances. In the October 1999 General Session, the MPR votes by secret ballot on whether to accept ([i]Menerima[/i]) or reject ([i]Menolak[/i]) Habibie's speech under TAP MPR No. III/MPR/1999. Historically, Habibie's speech was rejected 355 to 322 on the night of October 19-20, 1999, forcing him to withdraw his presidential candidacy.",
	"lib.lib_079.subtitle": "Presidential Accountability Speech to the MPR General Session",
	"lib.lib_079.term": "Pidato Pertanggungjawaban",
	"lib.lib_080.desc": "A Sidang Istimewa (Special Session) is an extraordinary sitting of the MPR convened outside the normal five-year cycle; the November 10-13, 1998 Special Session shortened Habibie's caretaker term, scheduled the June 1999 elections, and saw the deadly Semanggi I clashes outside the building. The Sidang Umum (General Session) of October 1-21, 1999 was the full constitutional sitting of the newly seated 700-member MPR to judge Habibie's Accountability Speech and elect a President.",
	"lib.lib_080.subtitle": "The Extraordinary & General Sessions of the Assembly",
	"lib.lib_080.term": "Sidang Istimewa (SI) MPR 1998 & Sidang Umum (SU) MPR 1999",
	"lib.lib_081.desc": "Irama Tersedak ('Choked Rhythm') was a playful Jakarta acronym for [i]Irian, Maluku, Ternate, Sulawesi, Manado, Makassar[/i], referring to the Eastern Indonesian caucus inside Golkar (led by A.A. Baramuli, Marwah Daud Ibrahim, and Fadel Muhammad) that fiercely championed fellow Sulawesi native B.J. Habibie. Media commentators also divided the party into Golkar Putih ('White Golkar', Habibie's ICMI and reformist wing) and Golkar Hitam ('Black Golkar', the pragmatic New Order party machine loyal to Akbar Tandjung).",
	"lib.lib_081.subtitle": "Inside-Golkar Factional Slang of the 1999 MPR Session",
	"lib.lib_081.term": "Irama Tersedak & Golkar Putih vs. Golkar Hitam",
	"lib.lib_082.desc": "Under the New Order, every newspaper and magazine required a SIUPP license from the Ministry of Information, which could be revoked without trial if a publication crossed regime red lines (as happened to [i]Tempo[/i], [i]Editor[/i], and [i]DeTIK[/i] in 1994). In June 1998, Habibie's Information Minister Lt. Gen. Yunus Yosfiah abolished the draconian SIUPP restrictions, ushering in an explosion of press freedom and culminating in the landmark Press Law (Law No. 40/1999).",
	"lib.lib_082.subtitle": "Press Publication Business License: The New Order's Censorship Tool",
	"lib.lib_082.term": "SIUPP (Surat Izin Usaha Penerbitan Pers)",
	"lib.lib_083.desc": "A legal order issued by the Attorney General's Office halting a criminal prosecution due to purported 'insufficient evidence.' After a leaked February 1999 phone recording caught President Habibie and Attorney General Andi Muhammad Ghalib discussing the Suharto corruption probe in staged, deferential terms, the issuance of an SP3 dropping the investigation into former President Suharto on October 11, 1999, just days before the MPR General Session, sparked outrage among reformers while pleasing old-guard Golkar and Cendana brokers.",
	"lib.lib_083.subtitle": "Formal Order Terminating a Criminal Investigation",
	"lib.lib_083.term": "SP3 (Surat Perintah Penghentian Penyidikan)",
	"lib.lib_084.desc": "The famous and physically missing document signed by President Sukarno under military pressure on March 11, 1966, authorizing Lt. Gen. Suharto to take 'all measures deemed necessary' to guarantee security and stability. Suharto used it to ban the PKI, arrest cabinet ministers, and seize executive power. In 1998-1999, invoking a 'Supersemar II' or martial-law decree was the ultimate authoritarian temptation whispered by hardliners.",
	"lib.lib_084.subtitle": "The Order of March 11, 1966: Emergency Transfer of Executive Power",
	"lib.lib_084.term": "Supersemar (Surat Perintah Sebelas Maret)",
	"lib.lib_085.desc": "Two foundational pillars of New Order depoliticization. Massa Mengambang ('Floating Mass', 1973) banned opposition parties (PPP and PDI) from organizing below the district level so villagers would remain 'uncorrupted' by politics between five-year elections, while Golkar operated freely through the village bureaucracy. Asas Tunggal ('Sole Foundation', 1985) forced all political parties and civic organizations to adopt state-defined Pancasila as their sole ideology. Both were repealed under Habibie's 1999 political laws.",
	"lib.lib_085.subtitle": "Ideological Uniformity & 'Floating Mass' Doctrines of the New Order",
	"lib.lib_085.term": "Asas Tunggal & Massa Mengambang",
	"lib.lib_086.desc": "Established in 1971 as the compulsory sole association for all four million Indonesian civil servants, teachers, and state enterprise employees. Under the doctrine of monoloyalitas (monoloyalty), all KORPRI members and their families were required to vote and campaign exclusively for Golkar. In early 1999, Habibie enacted Government Regulation No. 5/1999 establishing civil-service neutrality, cutting off Golkar's automatic bureaucratic vote machine on the eve of the June election.",
	"lib.lib_086.subtitle": "Civil Servants Corps of the Republic of Indonesia",
	"lib.lib_086.term": "KORPRI & Monoloyalitas",
	"lib.lib_087.desc": "SBKRI ([i]Surat Bukti Kewarganegaraan Republik Indonesia[/i]) forced ethnic Chinese Indonesians to carry special citizenship certificates to obtain passports, marriage licenses, or university admission. Following the 1965-1966 anti-communist purges, hundreds of thousands of former political detainees had 'ET' ([i]Eks-Tapol[/i]) stamped on their ID cards, while civil servants, journalists, and candidates faced Litsus ('Special Research') ideological screening. Tapol / Napol referred to political detainees and prisoners held in Cipinang Prison, whom Habibie began releasing in May 1998.",
	"lib.lib_087.subtitle": "New Order Discriminatory, Ideological Screening & Detention Terms",
	"lib.lib_087.term": "SBKRI, Litsus & Eks-Tapol (Tapol / Napol)",
	"lib.lib_088.desc": "Pancasila is the five-principle philosophical foundation of the Indonesian state proclaimed by Sukarno in June 1945: Belief in the One and Only God, a just and civilized humanity, the unity of Indonesia, democracy guided by the inner wisdom of deliberation among representatives, and social justice for all the people of Indonesia. Orde Lama ('Old Order') refers to Sukarno's 1945-1966 presidency, while Orde Baru ('New Order', or [i]Orba[/i]) refers to Suharto's 1966-1998 military-backed developmentalist regime, succeeded in May 1998 by the Era Reformasi.",
	"lib.lib_088.subtitle": "Foundational State Philosophy & Regime Epochs of Modern Indonesia",
	"lib.lib_088.term": "Pancasila, Orde Lama & Orde Baru",
	"lib.lib_089.desc": "Coined by anthropologist Clifford Geertz, Aliran ('streams') describes the deep cultural-religious cleavages dividing Indonesian voters between secular or syncretic nationalists ([i]abangan[/i], the core base of PDI-P) and pious Muslims ([i]santri[/i], split between traditionalist NU/PKB and modernist Muhammadiyah/PAN/PPP). Wong Cilik ('little people') refers to the urban and rural working poor (pedicab drivers, street vendors, and smallholders) who formed Megawati's devoted grassroots following. Cukong refers to wealthy ethnic Chinese business tycoons (such as Liem Sioe Liong and Bob Hasan) who partnered with New Order generals and the Suharto family in exchange for state monopolies and political protection.",
	"lib.lib_089.subtitle": "Sociological Fault Lines & Class Terms in Indonesian Politics",
	"lib.lib_089.term": "Aliran, Wong Cilik & Cukong",
	"lib.pers_000.desc": "Born in Surakarta (Solo), Central Java, in 1944 into a devout modernist Muslim family, Muhammad Amien Rais earned a political science doctorate from the University of Chicago before teaching at Gadjah Mada University in Yogyakarta. Briefly an early member of Habibie's ICMI think tank, he was forced out after criticizing foreign mining contracts at Freeport and Busang, and in 1995 became Chairman of Muhammadiyah, Indonesia's second-largest Islamic organization. In early 1998, as the rupiah collapsed, he emerged as the most fearless national figure demanding Suharto's resignation and calling for a total campaign against [i]Korupsi, Kolusi, dan Nepotisme[/i] (KKN).\n\nExpecting his prominence in the May 1998 uprising to translate into sweeping electoral support, Amien founded the pluralist National Mandate Party (PAN) in August 1998, but finished a disappointing fifth in the June 1999 election with just 7.1% of the vote and 34 DPR seats. Refusing to be sidelined, he engineered the Poros Tengah (Central Axis)—a loose alliance of modernist and traditionalist Islamic parties—to block both a second term for Habibie and a presidency for secular-nationalist frontrunner Megawati Sukarnoputri. By cutting deals with Akbar Tandjung's Golkar faction and General Wiranto in early October 1999, Amien won election as Speaker of the MPR, presiding over the rejection of Habibie's Accountability Speech and the surprise election of Gus Dur.\n\nLess than two years after installing Gus Dur in the palace, Amien turned against him and spearheaded the July 2001 MPR Special Session that removed Gus Dur and elevated Megawati. As MPR Speaker through 2004, he oversaw the four historic constitutional amendments that stripped the MPR of its presidential-selection powers and established direct presidential elections. After finishing fourth in the 2004 presidential race, he remained an outspoken political gadfly, breaking with PAN in 2020 following internal leadership disputes to found the minor Ummat Party.",
	"lib.pers_000.subtitle": "b. 1944, Surakarta, Central Java — Founder of PAN, Architect of the Central Axis & Speaker of the MPR (1999–2004)",
	"lib.pers_000.term": "Amien Rais",
	"lib.pers_001.desc": "Born in Sibolga, North Sumatra, in 1945, Akbar Tandjung rose through the Muslim Students' Association (HMI) in Jakarta during the 1966 student demonstrations that helped bring Suharto to power. A patient party organizer and veteran of three consecutive Suharto cabinets (serving as Minister of Youth and Sports and Minister of Public Housing), he served as President Habibie's Minister/State Secretary while winning a fierce battle against retired Gen. Edi Sudradjat at Golkar's July 1998 Extraordinary Congress to become Chairman of the newly reorganized Golkar Party.\n\nTasked with salvaging the New Order's hated electoral machine, Akbar kept Golkar alive in the June 1999 election, securing second place with 22.4% of the vote (120 DPR seats). Behind the scenes, however, Golkar fractured between Habibie's Sulawesi/ICMI loyalists ('White Golkar') and Akbar's pragmatic Slipi party apparatus ('Black Golkar'). Recognizing that Bank Bali and East Timor had made Habibie toxic to the public and the military, Akbar quietly negotiated a vote-trading pact with Amien Rais and Gus Dur. On the night of October 19–20, 1999, dozens of Akbar's delegates defected behind the secret-ballot curtain to vote down Habibie's Accountability Speech, while Akbar secured the speakership of the DPR for himself.\n\nIn 2001, prosecutors revealed that while serving as Habibie's State Secretary in early 1999, Akbar had signed off on Rp 40 billion in off-budget poverty-relief funds from the state logistics agency (Bulog) to a shadowy foundation that whistleblowers alleged had bankrolled Golkar's 1999 campaign. Convicted of corruption and sentenced to three years in prison in 2002, Akbar refused to resign as DPR Speaker while appealing, and in February 2004 the Supreme Court controversially overturned his conviction 4–1. After losing the Golkar chairmanship to Jusuf Kalla later that year, he settled into the role of senior party patriarch, chairing Golkar's Advisory and Honorary Councils through the 2020s.",
	"lib.pers_001.subtitle": "b. 1945, Sibolga, North Sumatra — Chairman of the Golkar Party (1998–2004) & Speaker of the DPR (1999–2004)",
	"lib.pers_001.term": "Akbar Tandjung",
	"lib.pers_002.desc": "Born in Yogyakarta in 1947 and raised in Surakarta, Wiranto graduated from the National Military Academy in 1968 and rose to prominence as Suharto's presidential adjutant from 1989 to 1993. Promoted rapidly through the Jakarta Garrison (Kodam Jaya), Kostrad, and Army Chief of Staff, he was appointed Commander of the Armed Forces (ABRI) in February 1998. During the May 1998 crisis, Wiranto refused calls from hardliners to crush student protesters at the parliament complex with martial force, advised Suharto to step down while pledging the military would protect the Suharto family, and promptly sidelined his chief rival, Kostrad commander Lt. Gen. Prabowo Subianto, the day after Habibie took office.\n\nServing concurrently as Armed Forces Commander and Minister of Defense and Security throughout Habibie's presidency, Wiranto navigated a perilous balancing act. He oversaw the fatal shootings of unarmed student protesters at Semanggi I (November 1998—where the military mobilized bamboo-spear-wielding [i]Pam Swakarsa[/i] civilian militias) and Semanggi II (September 1999), while officers under his command armed and funded pro-integration militias in East Timor that carried out a scorched-earth campaign after the August 1999 independence ballot. At the October 1999 MPR General Session, after quietly entertaining and then declining Habibie's last-minute offer of the vice-presidential nomination, Wiranto kept the 38-seat military bloc autonomous as Habibie's speech went down to defeat.\n\nProtected by a 2001 DPR Special Committee vote declaring that the Trisakti and Semanggi shootings were not gross human rights violations, and shielded by Jakarta from a 2003 UN-backed indictment for crimes against humanity in East Timor, Wiranto never faced trial. He founded the Hanura Party in 2006, ran for President in 2004 and Vice President in 2009, and returned to executive power under President Joko Widodo as Coordinating Minister for Political, Legal, and Security Affairs (2016–2019) and Chairman of the Presidential Advisory Council (2019–2024), before being appointed Special Advisor to the President for Political and Security Affairs by President Prabowo Subianto in 2024.",
	"lib.pers_002.subtitle": "b. 1947, Yogyakarta — Commander of ABRI/TNI & Minister of Defense (1998–1999)",
	"lib.pers_002.term": "Wiranto",
	"lib.pers_003.desc": "Born in Makassar, South Sulawesi, in 1952, Sjafrie Sjamsoeddin graduated from the Military Academy in 1974 in the same class as his close Kopassus associate Prabowo Subianto and spent years inside Suharto's personal presidential security detail (Paspampres). In late 1997, he was appointed Commander of Kodam Jaya, the vital military garrison directly responsible for the security of Jakarta during the collapse of the New Order.\n\nDuring the May 12, 1998 Trisakti shootings and the subsequent three days of May 13–15 rioting—in which organized provocateurs torched more than 4,000 buildings, over a thousand people died in burning shopping malls, and hundreds of ethnic Chinese-Indonesian women were gang-raped—Kodam Jaya forces were conspicuously absent from key districts of the capital. On May 14, Sjafrie famously rode an armored personnel carrier through central Jakarta to calm crowds and attended the controversial meeting at Kostrad headquarters alongside Prabowo. Reassigned out of troop command after Suharto's fall, Sjafrie attributed the May security vacuum to communications breakdowns before the TGPF fact-finding team and later declined to appear under subpoena when Komnas HAM investigated the May 1998 unrest.\n\nBecause the Attorney General's Office repeatedly returned Komnas HAM's May 1998 investigative files without convening a human rights court, Sjafrie's career continued unimpeded. He served as TNI Chief Spokesperson in 2002, Secretary-General of the Ministry of Defense (2005–2010), and Deputy Minister of Defense (2010–2014) under President Yudhoyono. Brought back to the Defense Ministry as special advisor to Prabowo Subianto in 2019, Sjafrie was appointed Minister of Defense in October 2024 and awarded an honorary four-star General rank in 2025.",
	"lib.pers_003.subtitle": "b. 1952, Makassar, South Sulawesi — 1998 Jakarta Garrison Commander (Pangdam Jaya) & Minister of Defense",
	"lib.pers_003.term": "Sjafrie Sjamsoeddin",
	"lib.pers_004.desc": "Born in Ketapang, West Kalimantan, in 1940, Hamzah Haz began his career as a journalist and economics lecturer in Pontianak and rose through Nahdlatul Ulama's regional ranks before entering parliament in 1971. When the New Order forcibly merged four Islamic parties into the United Development Party (PPP) in 1973, Hamzah became a fixture on the DPR budget committee, mastering parliamentary procedure while surviving within Suharto's tightly policed opposition.\n\nAppointed Minister of Investment in Habibie's Development Reform Cabinet in May 1998, Hamzah won the chairmanship of PPP at the party's late-1998 congress, restoring the [i]Ka'bah[/i] as the party's symbol and Islam as its ideological foundation. Stepping down from the cabinet in May 1999 to lead PPP into the June election, he captured 58 seats in the MPR, making PPP the largest Islamic party in parliament. Though initially sympathetic to Habibie, Hamzah joined Amien Rais's Central Axis in mid-1999, voiced public reservations about a female president to block Megawati, and swung PPP's decisive bloc behind Gus Dur after Habibie's Accountability Speech was rejected.\n\nAfter a brief tenure in Gus Dur's first cabinet, Hamzah became a leading parliamentary critic of Gus Dur's presidency. When the MPR removed Gus Dur in July 2001 and elevated Megawati Sukarnoputri, Hamzah promptly abandoned his earlier objections to a female head of state and defeated Akbar Tandjung and Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono in an MPR floor vote to become the ninth Vice President of Indonesia (2001–2004). He led PPP until 2007 and lived quietly in Jakarta and Bogor until his death in July 2024 at age 84.",
	"lib.pers_004.subtitle": "b. 1940, Ketapang, West Kalimantan (d. 2024) — Chairman of PPP (1998–2007) & 9th Vice President of Indonesia (2001–2004)",
	"lib.pers_004.term": "Hamzah Haz",
	"lib.pers_005.desc": "Born on the island of Belitung in 1956, Yusril Ihza Mahendra studied constitutional law and philosophy at the University of Indonesia and earned his doctorate in Malaysia before joining Suharto's State Secretariat as a speechwriter in the late 1990s. In May 1998, as student protesters occupied the parliament complex, it was Yusril who drafted Suharto's resignation announcement and advised B.J. Habibie on the constitutional mechanics of his immediate swearing-in at Merdeka Palace.\n\nTwo months later, in July 1998, Yusril founded the modernist Islamic Crescent Star Party (PBB), styling it as the spiritual and legal heir to the 1950s Masyumi party. Winning 13 seats in the June 1999 election, Yusril became a key constitutional strategist inside Amien Rais's Central Axis during the October 1999 MPR General Session, even standing briefly as a presidential candidate on October 20 before withdrawing at the last minute to consolidate Islamic party votes behind Gus Dur.\n\nRenowned for his mastery of statutory law, Yusril made himself indispensable to nearly every post-Reformasi administration, serving successively as Minister of Law and Legislation under Gus Dur, Minister of Justice and Human Rights under Megawati, and State Secretary under Yudhoyono. Named a corruption suspect by the Attorney General's Office in 2008 over the Ministry of Law's online corporate registry fee system ([i]Sisminbakum[/i]), he countersued in the Constitutional Court and had the case formally terminated via SP3 in 2012. After leading the legal defense team for Prabowo Subianto in the 2014, 2019, and 2024 presidential election disputes, Yusril was appointed Coordinating Minister for Law, Human Rights, Immigration, and Corrections in October 2024.",
	"lib.pers_005.subtitle": "b. 1956, Belitung — Founder of the Crescent Star Party (PBB) & Coordinating Minister for Law and Human Rights",
	"lib.pers_005.term": "Yusril Ihza Mahendra",
	"lib.pers_006.desc": "Born in Bandung, West Java, in 1941 into a prominent Sundanese nationalist family, Ginandjar Kartasasmita studied chemical engineering in Tokyo, joined the Indonesian Air Force (rising to the rank of Air Marshal), and entered Suharto's economic bureaucracy as head of the Investment Coordinating Board (BKPM), Minister of Mines and Energy, and head of the National Development Planning Agency (Bappenas). Unlike the orthodox US-trained 'Berkeley Mafia' economists, Ginandjar championed economic nationalism and state support for indigenous ([i]pribumi[/i]) entrepreneurs.\n\nOn the night of May 20, 1998, meeting inside the Bappenas building as Jakarta smoldered, Ginandjar led thirteen fellow economic ministers in signing the historic letter refusing to serve in Suharto's proposed 'Reform Cabinet'—the decisive blow that forced Suharto to resign the following morning. Retained by President Habibie as Coordinating Minister for Economy, Finance, and Industry (Menko Ekuin), Ginandjar spent 1998–1999 renegotiating Letters of Intent with the IMF, overseeing the painful restructuring of Indonesia's collapsed banking sector through IBRA, and fending off opposition accusations over his past ties to Freeport mining contracts and Baligate.\n\nSurviving the fall of Habibie's administration unscathed, Ginandjar remained a senior Golkar powerbroker in the MPR, helping steer the constitutional amendments of 1999–2002. Following the creation of Indonesia's new upper regional chamber, he was elected the first Speaker of the Regional Representative Council (DPD RI) from 2004 to 2009 and later served on President Yudhoyono's Presidential Advisory Council (Wantimpres) from 2010 to 2014.",
	"lib.pers_006.subtitle": "b. 1941, Bandung, West Java — Coordinating Minister for Economy, Finance & Industry (1998–1999)",
	"lib.pers_006.term": "Ginandjar Kartasasmita",
	"lib.pers_007.desc": "Born in Pekalongan, Central Java, in 1943, Adi Sasono was a student activist at the Bandung Institute of Technology (ITB) in the 1960s before spending two decades as a community development NGO organizer and social thinker. In 1990, he helped B.J. Habibie establish the Association of Indonesian Muslim Intellectuals (ICMI), becoming its Secretary-General in 1995 and forging the bridge between Habibie's technocratic circle and urban Muslim activists.\n\nAppointed Minister of Cooperatives and Small and Medium Enterprises in Habibie's May 1998 cabinet, Adi Sasono became the administration's most polarizing populist figure. Championing a doctrine he called Ekonomi Kerakyatan ('People's Economy'), he pushed to dismantle ethnic Chinese conglomerate monopolies, redistribute seized BLBI forestry and plantation assets to rural cooperatives, and channel over Rp 10 trillion in subsidized central-bank credit schemes across the archipelago. While grassroots cooperatives hailed him as a Robin Hood figure, IMF officials, orthodox technocrats, and rival parties accused him of building a massive state-funded patronage network to secure Habibie's re-election in the MPR.\n\nAfter Habibie's defeat in October 1999, Adi Sasono left government, briefly founded the Freedom Bull National Party (Partai Merdeka), and later returned to chair the Indonesian Dekopin cooperative council until his death in Jakarta in August 2016.",
	"lib.pers_007.subtitle": "b. 1943, Pekalongan, Central Java (d. 2016) — Minister of Cooperatives & Champion of 'Ekonomi Kerakyatan'",
	"lib.pers_007.term": "Adi Sasono",
	"lib.pers_008.desc": "Born in Surakarta, Central Java, in 1943, Muladi studied law at Diponegoro University in Semarang, where he served as rector from 1994 to 1998 while sitting on the National Commission on Human Rights (Komnas HAM). Tapped by Suharto in March 1998 as Minister of Justice and retained by President Habibie in May 1998, Muladi became the primary legal workhorse of the transition cabinet, taking on the additional portfolio of Minister/State Secretary in May 1999 after Akbar Tandjung stepped down to campaign for Golkar.\n\nWorking at breakneck speed from Bina Graha and the Justice Ministry, Muladi drafted the presidential decrees releasing dozens of New Order political prisoners (including Muchtar Pakpahan and Sri Bintang Pamungkas), piloted the repeal of the 1963 Anti-Subversion Law, and steered the landmark 1999 Human Rights Act and 'One-Roof' ([i]Satu Atap[/i]) judicial independence amendment through parliament. At the same time, as a loyal defender of Habibie and Golkar, he frequently clashed with student demonstrators and human rights NGOs over the government's handling of the Suharto corruption inquiry and the Bank Bali audit.\n\nAfter leaving the cabinet in October 1999, Muladi served briefly as a Supreme Court Justice (2000–2001) and was appointed Governor of the National Resilience Institute (Lemhannas) by President Yudhoyono from 2005 to 2011, remaining an influential voice in criminal code reform until his death in Jakarta in December 2020.",
	"lib.pers_008.subtitle": "b. 1943, Surakarta, Central Java (d. 2020) — Minister of Justice & Minister/State Secretary (1998–1999)",
	"lib.pers_008.term": "Muladi",
	"lib.pers_009.desc": "Hailing from President Habibie's home province of South Sulawesi, Attorney General Lt. Gen. Andi Muhammad Ghalib (born in Bone in 1946) and Supreme Advisory Council (DPA) Chairman Arnold Achmad Baramuli (born in Pinrang in 1930) formed the core of Habibie's regional palace loyalists, nicknamed the [i]Irama Tersedak[/i] caucus. Appointed Attorney General in June 1998 to lead the anti-KKN probe into former President Suharto, Ghalib instead saw his credibility collapse in February 1999 when the press published a wiretapped telephone conversation showing Habibie and Ghalib choreographing a deferential, pre-scripted questioning of Suharto. Four months later, anti-graft watchdogs exposed hundreds of millions of rupiah in checks from conglomerate tycoons flowing into bank accounts managed by Ghalib as head of the Indonesian Wrestling Association, forcing him to step aside in June 1999.\n\nSimultaneously, Baramuli—a former prosecutor, provincial governor, and wealthy Golkar industrialist who headed Habibie's informal [i]Tim Sukses[/i] re-election operation—became the central figure in the Bank Bali ('Baligate') scandal after parliamentary hearings and audits revealed he had pressured Bank Bali owner Rudy Ramli and IBRA officials over the Rp 546 billion commission paid to Golkar insiders.\n\nNeither man ever served prison time. Military and civilian prosecutors accepted Ghalib's explanation that the tycoon payments were purely athletic donations, while acting Attorney General Ismudjoko issued an SP3 dropping the Suharto probe on October 11, 1999. Baramuli's Bank Bali indictment bogged down in court on procedural challenges until his death in 2006, while Ghalib was later appointed Indonesian Ambassador to India (2008–2009) and elected to parliament for PPP before dying in 2016 and receiving a full military burial at Kalibata Heroes Cemetery.",
	"lib.pers_009.subtitle": "Ghalib (b. 1946, Bone; d. 2016) & Baramuli (b. 1930, Pinrang; d. 2006) — Habibie's South Sulawesi Inner Circle",
	"lib.pers_009.term": "Andi Muhammad Ghalib & A.A. Baramuli",
	"lib.pers_010.desc": "Born in Bandung in 1955, Golkar deputy treasurer Setya Novanto partnered in early 1999 with Kalimantan-born property tycoon Djoko Soegiarto Tjandra (b. 1951, Sanggau) through their company PT Era Giat Prima (EGP) to extract a Rp 546 billion ($70 million) 'collection fee' from state-supervised Bank Bali—nearly 60% of the Rp 904 billion interbank claim guaranteed by Indonesian taxpayers via IBRA. When a PricewaterhouseCoopers (PwC) forensic audit uncovered that the funds were funneled to Golkar and Palace operators ahead of the October 1999 MPR presidential vote, the resulting 'Baligate' scandal prompted the IMF to freeze bailout disbursements and fatally crippled Habibie's re-election bid.\n\nBoth men escaped accountability for decades. In 2000, the South Jakarta District Court acquitted Djoko Tjandra on grounds that the Bank Bali cessie agreement was a civil matter, while the Attorney General's Office issued an SP3 in 2003 terminating the investigation into Setya Novanto. When the Supreme Court overturned Tjandra's acquittal on special review in June 2009 and sentenced him to two years in prison, Tjandra chartered a private jet out of Jakarta the day before the ruling and lived for eleven years as a fugitive billionaire in Papua New Guinea and Malaysia.\n\nUnscathed by Bank Bali, Setya Novanto rose to become Chairman of the Golkar Party (2016–2017) and Speaker of the DPR (2014–2015, 2016–2017). Only in November 2017—after famously staging a car crash into a utility pole to evade KPK investigators—was he arrested and sentenced to 15 years in prison for the Rp 2.3 trillion electronic ID card (e-KTP) corruption scheme; in 2025, the Supreme Court cut his sentence to 12.5 years on judicial review, allowing him to walk free on conditional parole from Sukamiskin Prison in August 2025. Djoko Tjandra was eventually captured in Malaysia in July 2020 after a second scandal revealed he had bribed two police generals and a state prosecutor to erase his Interpol Red Notice.",
	"lib.pers_010.subtitle": "Novanto (b. 1955, Bandung) & Tjandra (b. 1951, Sanggau, West Kalimantan) — Principals of the 1999 Bank Bali Scandal",
	"lib.pers_010.term": "Setya Novanto & Djoko S. Tjandra",
	"lib.pers_011.desc": "Formed in mid-1997 inside Group IV of the Army Special Forces (Kopassus) at Cijantung under Group Commander Col. Chairawan and overall Kopassus Commander Maj. Gen. Prabowo Subianto, Tim Mawar ('Rose Team') was an eleven-man covert intelligence unit led in the field by Maj. Inf. Bambang Kristiono (born in Purwakarta in 1960). Between late 1997 and May 1998, Tim Mawar operatives abducted and interrogated more than twenty student, labor, and pro-democracy activists across Java and Sumatra; while nine activists (including Andi Arief, Desmond J. Mahesa, and Pius Lustrilanang) were eventually released alive from secret cells, thirteen others—including poet Wiji Thukul, Suyat, and Herman Hendrawan—were forcibly disappeared and never found.\n\nWhen eleven Tim Mawar members were brought before High Military Court II in Jakarta in early 1999, military prosecutors restricted the indictment exclusively to the nine kidnapping victims who survived, omitting any charges regarding the thirteen missing activists or higher chain-of-command orders. In April 1999, the court convicted the officers of procedural 'insubordination,' handing down sentences of 12 to 22 months and ordering five officers dismissed from military service. None of the eleven ever served prison time: they remained on active duty while appealing to the Supreme Court, which quietly overturned their dismissals in 2007.\n\nEvery surviving officer went on to reach two-star or three-star general rank in the TNI and later entered senior civilian government roles in the 2020s: Lt. Gen. Djaka Budhi Utama (b. 1967, Jakarta) served as Inspector General of the Ministry of Defense and Principal Secretary of BIN before being appointed Director General of Customs and Excise at the Ministry of Finance in May 2025; Lt. Gen. (Ret.) Nugroho Sulistiyo Budi (b. 1967, Pekalongan) became Head of the National Cyber and Crypto Agency (BSSN) in February 2025; Maj. Gen. (Ret.) Yulius Selvanus (b. 1963, Tana Toraja) was inaugurated as Governor of North Sulawesi in February 2025; Maj. Gen. (Ret.) Dadang Hendrayudha (b. 1965, Karawang) became a senior deputy at the National Nutrition Agency in 2024; and unit commander Bambang Kristiono served as a senior Gerindra MP and Deputy Chair of DPR Commission I until his death in July 2023.",
	"lib.pers_011.subtitle": "Commanded by Maj. Bambang Kristiono (b. 1960, Purwakarta; d. 2023) with Capt. Djaka Budhi Utama (b. 1967, Jakarta) & Fellow Officers",
	"lib.pers_011.term": "Tim Mawar (The Kopassus Group IV Abduction Squad)",
	"lib.pers_012.desc": "During the 1997–1998 financial meltdown, Suharto's closest corporate conglomerates received over Rp 144.5 trillion in emergency Bank Indonesia liquidity support (BLBI) to cover bank runs—funds that a 2000 Supreme Audit Agency (BPK) investigation concluded were 95% diverted into affiliated group companies, currency speculation, and offshore accounts. The largest recipients were the Salim Group's Bank Central Asia, led by Anthony Salim (born in Kudus in 1949, son of New Order business patriarch Liem Sioe Liong), which absorbed Rp 52.7 trillion; Bank Dagang Negara Indonesia (BDNI), owned by tire and retail magnate Sjamsul Nursalim (born in Tanjung Karang, Lampung, in 1942), which absorbed Rp 28.4 trillion; and Bank Umum Nasional, co-owned by Suharto's golfing partner and timber monopoly kingpin Bob Hasan (born in Semarang in 1931).\n\nWhile Bob Hasan was convicted in 2001 of defrauding the Ministry of Forestry's aerial mapping fund and served three years in Nusa Kambangan prison before his parole in 2004, the largest bank owners avoided prison by signing Master Settlement and Acquisition Agreements (MSAA) with IBRA in late 1998. Under those agreements, Anthony Salim and Sjamsul Nursalim surrendered corporate shares and industrial assets—later valued at a fraction of their claimed worth—in exchange for 'Release and Discharge' immunity, reinforced by formal Release Letters (SKL) issued under President Megawati in 2002–2004.\n\nWhen the Corruption Eradication Commission (KPK) belatedly indicted former IBRA chief Syafruddin Arsyad Temenggung and Sjamsul Nursalim in 2017–2019 over a Rp 4.58 trillion state loss from BDNI's fictitious shrimp-farmer receivables, the Supreme Court acquitted Temenggung in July 2019 on grounds that the act was not criminal, and in April 2021 the KPK issued its first-ever SP3 terminating all charges against Nursalim. Bob Hasan died in Jakarta in March 2020, while Anthony Salim and Sjamsul Nursalim remain among the wealthiest billionaires in Southeast Asia, controlling vast food, mining, retail, property, and infrastructure empires across Indonesia and Singapore.",
	"lib.pers_012.subtitle": "Nursalim (b. 1942, Lampung), Salim (b. 1949, Kudus) & Hasan (b. 1931, Semarang; d. 2020)",
	"lib.pers_012.term": "Sjamsul Nursalim, Anthony Salim & Bob Hasan (The BLBI Tycoons)",
	"lib.pers_013.desc": "Born in Uatulari, Portuguese Timor, in 1971, Eurico Guterres was orphaned during the 1970s conflict and recruited by Indonesian military intelligence into the pro-integration youth movement [i]Gada Paksi[/i] before taking command of the Dili-based Aitarak ('Thorn') militia in early 1999. Operating alongside territorial troops under Kodam IX/Udayana Commander Maj. Gen. Adam Damiri (born in Garut, West Java, in 1949), Aitarak and allied pro-Indonesia militias waged a campaign of intimidation ahead of the August 30, 1999 UN referendum, culminating in the April 1999 massacre at Manuel Carrascalão's residence in Dili and the September 1999 scorched-earth destruction of East Timor after 78.5% of voters chose independence.\n\nTo head off a UN Security Council international tribunal, Jakarta created a domestic Ad Hoc Human Rights Court for East Timor in 2002. Of the eighteen military, police, and civilian figures tried, twelve were acquitted outright and five—including Maj. Gen. Adam Damiri and all other Indonesian military and police officers convicted—had their sentences overturned on appeal. Only Eurico Guterres, an East Timorese civilian, served two years in Sukamiskin Prison (2006–2008) before the Supreme Court vacated his conviction on judicial review in April 2008 and ordered his immediate release.\n\nAdam Damiri retired with full military honors, while Eurico Guterres settled in Indonesia as head of the pro-Jakarta veterans' association [i]Uni Timor Aswain (UNTAS)[/i], joined PAN and later the Gerindra Party, and in August 2021 was personally awarded the Bintang Jasa Utama—one of Indonesia's highest civilian medals of merit—at Istana Negara by President Joko Widodo.",
	"lib.pers_013.subtitle": "Guterres (b. 1971, Uatulari, East Timor) & Damiri (b. 1949, Garut, West Java) — 1999 East Timor Campaign Figures",
	"lib.pers_013.term": "Eurico Guterres & Maj. Gen. Adam Damiri",
	"lib.pres_000.alt": "If you still worship the sepia-toned myth of Bung Karno as a faultless revolutionary god, ask the starving peasants of 1965 how many three-hour anti-imperialist speeches it takes to fill an empty stomach when hyperinflation hits 635%. The man who liberated you from Dutch chains could not stomach a parliament that talked back to him, so on July 5, 1959 he strangled Indonesia's young constitutional democracy in its cradle, crowned himself President-for-Life like a feudal Javanese monarch, and threw anti-colonial heroes like Sutan Sjahrir and Mochtar Lubis into prison without trial. While ordinary families queued for rotting food, he burned the republic's last foreign reserves on vanity monuments in Jakarta and a reckless military confrontation in Borneo. Worse still, his arrogant Nasakom tightrope act, pitting a paranoid Army against millions of unarmed PKI peasants and workers, lit the fuse for the 1965–1966 slaughter; and when the rivers of Java and Bali ran red with the blood of the very followers who adored him, the Great Leader of the Revolution sat in his palace bargaining for his own titles until Suharto locked him away to die.",
	"lib.pres_000.desc": "Born Kusno Sosrodihardjo in Surabaya, East Java, in 1901, Sukarno studied civil engineering at the [i]Technische Hoogeschool te Bandoeng[/i] (now ITB) before founding the Indonesian National Party (PNI) in 1927 to spearhead the anti-colonial struggle against Dutch rule. Imprisoned at Sukamiskin and exiled to Ende and Bengkulu for over a decade, he articulated the five foundational principles of Pancasila in June 1945 and, alongside Mohammad Hatta, proclaimed Indonesia's independence on August 17, 1945—holding a war-torn archipelago together through sheer oratorical magnetism across four years of armed revolution.\n\nYet Sukarno despised the messy compromises of the 1950s parliamentary era. Following regional rebellions and deadlock in the Constituent Assembly, he joined forces with the Army high command on July 5, 1959 to dissolve parliament by decree, reinstate the executive-heavy 1945 Constitution, and inaugurate Guided Democracy ([i]Demokrasi Terpimpin[/i]). Naming himself President-for-Life in 1963, he banned rival parties like Masyumi and PSI, silenced independent newspapers, and gambled the nation's survival on Nasakom—an increasingly volatile three-way balancing act between the Army, religious factions, and the three-million-strong Indonesian Communist Party (PKI) while the economy collapsed into 600% hyperinflation.\n\nThat cauldron exploded on the night of September 30, 1965, when the abortive G30S movement murdered six Army generals. Major General Suharto seized control of the military, unleashed the 1965–1966 anti-communist massacres that killed at least half a million Indonesians, and coerced Sukarno into signing the March 11, 1966 Supersemar order. Formally stripped of his presidential mandate by the Provisional MPR (MPRS) in March 1967 after its rejection of his [i]Nawaksara[/i] accountability address, the founding father was kept isolated under military house arrest at Wisma Yaso in Jakarta until his death in June 1970.",
	"lib.pres_000.subtitle": "b. 1901, Surabaya, East Java (d. 1970) — 1st President of the Republic of Indonesia (1945–1967)",
	"lib.pres_000.term": "Sukarno",
	"lib.pres_001.alt": "If you'd think Indonesia would've gone anywhere under the so-called \"Father of Development\" except straight into the offshore bank accounts of Jalan Cendana, you are spitting on every unmarked mass grave dug between 1965 and 1998. For thirty-two years, that grandfatherly Javanese smile masked a cold-blooded kleptocrat who built his throne on half a million anti-communist corpses, gagged every dissident and newspaper with the Anti-Subversion Law, turned Aceh and East Timor into military slaughterhouses, and carved up the republic's forests, banks, and toll roads like birthday cake for his six insatiable children and their crony tycoons. When the rot finally caved in during the 1997–1998 crash, tens of millions of ordinary Indonesians were thrown into destitution and over a thousand burned alive inside Jakarta's shopping malls while Rp 144.5 trillion in BLBI liquidity vanished into conglomerate pockets. Yet the most sickening obscenity is what you allowed to happen afterward. The old tyrant never spent a single second behind bars, his youngest son served barely four years for paying hitmen to murder a Supreme Court justice, his Kopassus kidnappers were rewarded with general stars and ministry portfolios, and the nation he bled dry got back on its knees in November 2025 to crown its own butcher a National Hero.",
	"lib.pres_001.desc": "Born in the village of Kemusuk, Yogyakarta, in 1921, Suharto rose through the Dutch colonial army (KNIL), the Japanese-sponsored PETA militia, and the revolutionary Indonesian Army, distinguishing himself in the March 1949 General Offensive in Yogyakarta. As commander of the Army Strategic Reserve (Kostrad) in October 1965, he crushed the September 30 Movement, extracted the March 11, 1966 Supersemar order from founding President Sukarno amid the 1965–1966 anti-communist purges, and ruled Indonesia for thirty-two years at the head of the military-backed New Order ([i]Orde Baru[/i]). Backed by the [color=__FAC_GOLKAR_A__]Golkar[/color] electoral machine, the military's Dwifungsi doctrine, and US-trained technocrats, his regime delivered decades of industrial growth and rice self-sufficiency while ruthlessly crushing labor unions, press freedom, and political dissent.\n\nFrom his family residence at No. 8 Jalan Cendana in Menteng, Suharto, his six children—led by Tutut (Siti Hardiyanti Rukmana, b. 1949), Bambang Trihatmodjo (b. 1953), Titiek (Siti Hediati Hariyadi, b. 1959), and Tommy (Hutomo Mandala Putra, b. 1962)—and seven tax-exempt presidential foundations (including [i]Supersemar[/i] and [i]Dakab[/i]) amassed an estimated $15 billion to $35 billion fortune through state monopolies in toll roads, cloves, oil trading, television, and the Timor national car. Forced to resign on May 21, 1998 after the Asian Financial Crisis and the May riots shattered his regime, Suharto retreated to Jalan Cendana under military protection. Although the November 1998 MPR Special Session explicitly named Suharto in TAP MPR No. XI/MPR/1998 as a target of anti-corruption investigations, Habibie's government issued an SP3 halting the probe in October 1999.\n\nWhen President Gus Dur reopened the prosecution in 2000, Suharto's doctors testified that a series of strokes had left him permanently unfit to stand trial, and in May 2006 the Attorney General's Office issued an SKP3 permanently dropping all criminal charges. Youngest son Tommy Suharto was convicted of a Rp 95 billion Bulog land-swap fraud in 2000, went underground, and paid hitmen to assassinate Supreme Court Justice Syafiuddin Kartasasmita in July 2001; sentenced to 15 years in 2002, he had his term cut to 10 years by the Supreme Court and walked free on parole in October 2006 after serving barely four years. Suharto died in January 2008 at age 86 with a full military state funeral, his name was formally scrubbed from TAP MPR No. XI/MPR/1998 in September 2024, and on November 10, 2025, his former son-in-law President Prabowo Subianto officially proclaimed him a National Hero (Pahlawan Nasional).",
	"lib.pres_001.subtitle": "b. 1921, Kemusuk, Yogyakarta (d. 2008) — 2nd President of the Republic of Indonesia (1967–1998)",
	"lib.pres_001.term": "Suharto",
	"lib.pres_002.alt": "Before you weep over movies painting B.J. Habibie as a misunderstood romantic genius and the sainted father of Indonesian democracy, ask yourself where his conscience was for the twenty-four years he spent feasting at Suharto's table. Calling the dictator his 'spiritual father' and praising him as [i]Super Genius Suharto[/i], Habibie happily raided Rp 400 billion from the Reforestation Fund to subsidize his pet IPTN aircraft factories while Indonesian villages lacked clean water, and built ICMI to give an aging autocracy an Islamic fig leaf. Even when history dumped the presidency into his lap in May 1998, every democratic reform he signed was wrung out of him by the threat of the students in the streets and a gun held to the treasury's head by the IMF. Behind closed doors, he was still Suharto's loyal apprentice, caught red-handed on a tapped phone line with Attorney General Andi Ghalib staging a fake, deferential corruption inquiry into Cendana, letting his palace [i]Tim Sukses[/i] loot Rp 546 billion from Bank Bali to buy MPR delegates, and casually tossing East Timor a referendum without lifting a finger to stop General Wiranto's militias from burning Dili to the ground.",
	"lib.pres_002.desc": "Born in Parepare, South Sulawesi, in 1936, Bacharuddin Jusuf Habibie lost his father at fourteen and won a scholarship to study aeronautical engineering at RWTH Aachen in West Germany, earning his doctorate [i]summa cum laude[/i] in 1965 and rising to become a vice president at Messerschmitt-Bölkow-Blohm. Summoned home by Suharto in 1974 to spearhead Indonesia's high-tech industrial drive, he served for twenty years as Minister of Research and Technology (1978–1998), pouring billions of state dollars into the IPTN aircraft complex in Bandung and chairing the Association of Indonesian Muslim Intellectuals (ICMI). Elevated from the Vice Presidency on May 21, 1998 upon Suharto's resignation, Suharto's lifelong protégé inherited a bankrupt treasury, a collapsed rupiah, and a republic on the edge of violent fracture.\n\nOver seventeen frantic months in office, the frenetic technocrat presided over the most rapid democratic opening in Indonesian history. Desperate to win domestic legitimacy and unlock IMF bailout tranches, he freed political prisoners, abolished SIUPP press censorship, legalized independent labor unions and dozens of political parties, emancipated Bank Indonesia, enacted sweeping Regional Autonomy, organized the June 7, 1999 multiparty election, and offered East Timor its August 1999 self-determination ballot. Yet his refusal to prosecute Suharto, exposed in a leaked February 1999 wiretap with Attorney General Andi Ghalib and sealed by an October 1999 SP3 termination order—combined with the Rp 546 billion Bank Bali ('Baligate') campaign slush-fund scandal and military fury over the loss of East Timor, proved fatal to his political survival.\n\nOn the night of October 19–20, 1999, after dozens of [color=__FAC_GOLKAR_A__]Golkar[/color] delegates loyal to party chairman Akbar Tandjung defected behind the secret-ballot curtain, the MPR rejected Habibie's Accountability Speech by a vote of 355 to 322, forcing him to withdraw his presidential candidacy before dawn. In retirement, he divided his time between Germany and Jakarta, founded the Habibie Center, and watched his public memory soften into that of an accidental democratic reformer before his death in September 2019 and burial at Kalibata Heroes Cemetery beside his wife, Hasri Ainun Besari.",
	"lib.pres_002.subtitle": "b. 1936, Parepare, South Sulawesi (d. 2019) | 3rd President of the Republic of Indonesia (1998–1999)",
	"lib.pres_002.term": "Bacharuddin Jusuf Habibie",
	"lib.pres_003.alt": "Strip away the untouchable halo of the saintly pluralist and look closely at how Gus Dur actually climbed into the presidential palace, and how recklessly he squandered the mandate of Reformasi once he got there. In 1998 and 1999, while students were still bleeding on the asphalt of Semanggi, this supposed moral conscience of the nation was busy paying friendly visits to Suharto at Jalan Cendana, hugging Megawati in public while sharpening knives behind her back with Amien Rais's sectarian [color=__FAC_AXIS__]Central Axis[/color], and riding the votes of [color=__FAC_GOLKAR_A__]Golkar[/color] and [color=__FAC_ABRI__]ABRI[/color] into office by letting clerics declare a woman unfit to lead. Once inside Istana Merdeka, he treated the republic like his private [i]pesantren[/i], announcing he would pardon Suharto if he ever went to trial, mocking the elected parliament as a 'kindergarten,' firing cabinet ministers almost every month, and shrugging while his personal masseur walked off with Rp 35 billion from Bulog alongside an undocumented $2 million gift from the Sultan of Brunei. And when his own coalition finally turned on him in July 2001, the great democrat tried to save his throne like a tinpot autocrat, ordering the military to dissolve parliament by midnight decree and handing the keys of the state straight back to the New Order sharks.",
	"lib.pres_003.desc": "Born in Jombang, East Java, in 1940 into the revered clerical dynasty that founded Nahdlatul Ulama (NU), Abdurrahman Wahid—universally known as Gus Dur—was educated at traditional Javanese [i]pesantren[/i] before studying in Cairo and Baghdad. Elected Chairman of NU in 1984, he led Indonesia's 40-million-strong traditionalist Muslim movement for fifteen years, championing religious pluralism, defending ethnic Chinese and Christian minorities, and infuriating both New Order hardliners and modernist ICMI rivals.\n\nDespite suffering a near-fatal stroke in early 1998 that left him legally blind, Gus Dur founded the [color=__FAC_PKB__]National Awakening Party (PKB)[/color] in July 1998, capturing 51 seats in the 1999 MPR. Throughout the transition, he bewildered allies and enemies alike with his erratic, Machiavellian maneuvers—visiting Suharto at Jalan Cendana to keep old-guard channels open, hosting the reformist Ciganjur Summit in November 1998, and publicly embracing Megawati Sukarnoputri while quietly allowing [color=__FAC_AXIS__]Amien Rais's Central Axis ([i]Poros Tengah[/i])[/color] to float his name as an Islamic compromise candidate. Following the rejection of Habibie's Accountability Speech on October 20, 1999, Gus Dur rode a backroom coalition of Islamic parties, [color=__FAC_GOLKAR_A__]Akbar Tandjung's Golkar[/color], and [color=__FAC_ABRI__]ABRI[/color] to defeat Megawati 373 to 313 and become Indonesia's fourth President.\n\nIn office, Gus Dur boldly abolished the Ministry of Information, repealed the thirty-year ban on Chinese cultural celebrations (Keppres No. 6/2000), apologized for the 1965–1966 anti-communist killings, allowed the Morning Star flag to fly in Papua, and forced General Wiranto out of his cabinet. Yet his chaotic administrative style, relentless cabinet sackings, and bitter clashes with the DPR over the Buloggate and Bruneigate financial scandals alienated the very coalition that installed him. Impeached by an MPR Special Session on July 23, 2001 hours after issuing a failed midnight decree ([i]Maklumat[/i]) to dissolve parliament and freeze Golkar, he stepped down peacefully and remained an iconic voice for minority rights until his death in December 2009, receiving the title of National Hero in November 2025.",
	"lib.pres_003.subtitle": "b. 1940, Jombang, East Java (d. 2009) — Founder of PKB & 4th President of the Republic of Indonesia (1999–2001)",
	"lib.pres_003.term": "Abdurrahman Wahid (Gus Dur)",
	"lib.pres_004.alt": "Ask the pedicab drivers, street vendors, and student activists who had their skulls cracked open by military truncheons on July 27, 1996 ([i]Kudatuli[/i]) what they actually got when Megawati Sukarnoputri finally ascended to her father's throne. Nothing. Nothing except the stony silence of a dynastic matriarch who believed her last name entitled her to the republic without lifting a finger for the [i]wong cilik[/i] who bled for her. Too arrogant to lower herself to parliamentary horse-trading in October 1999, she let Amien Rais and Akbar Tandjung steal the presidency right out from under her 33.7% plurality. And when she finally inherited the palace in 2001, the former icon of resistance governed like a conservative New Order regent, signing the infamous Release and Discharge (SKL) immunity letters via Inpres No. 8/2002 so Suharto's richest BLBI banking parasites could walk away with tens of trillions of stolen taxpayer rupiah, placing Aceh under full martial law, and rehabilitating the very generals who raided her own party headquarters in 1996, before spending the next twenty years running [color=__FAC_PDIP__]PDI-P[/color] like a feudal court where elected presidents are treated as mere 'party officers' ([i]petugas partai[/i]).",
	"lib.pres_004.desc": "Born in Yogyakarta in 1947 during the Indonesian National Revolution as the eldest daughter of founding President Sukarno and Fatmawati, Megawati Sukarnoputri spent her early adulthood outside politics after her father was deposed by Suharto in 1966. Drawn into parliament in 1987 by the Indonesian Democratic Party (PDI), she was elected PDI Chairwoman in 1993 in open defiance of the New Order's intelligence apparatus. Threatened by her immense grassroots appeal among the urban and rural poor ([i]wong cilik[/i]), the Suharto regime engineered a rival congress in Medan to oust her and backed the bloody July 27, 1996 raid on her party headquarters ([i]Peristiwa Kudatuli[/i]), turning her silent endurance into the premier symbol of civilian resistance.\n\nAfter Suharto's fall, she reorganized her movement as the [color=__FAC_PDIP__]Indonesian Democratic Party of Struggle (PDI-P)[/color] and swept the June 7, 1999 legislative election with 33.7% of the national vote and 153 seats in the MPR. Convinced that her electoral plurality entitled her to the presidency by birthright, Megawati remained aloof from the frantic coalition-building in Senayan, refusing to court Islamic parties or regional caucuses. During the October 1999 General Session, her caucus voted overwhelmingly to reject Habibie's Accountability Speech, only to be outmaneuvered days later when Amien Rais's [color=__FAC_AXIS__]Central Axis[/color] and Akbar Tandjung's [color=__FAC_GOLKAR_A__]Golkar[/color] bloc united behind Abdurrahman Wahid (Gus Dur), defeating her 373 to 313 and leaving her to accept the Vice Presidency to calm her rioting supporters across Java and Bali.\n\nWhen the MPR impeached and removed Gus Dur in July 2001, Megawati ascended to become Indonesia's fifth President (2001–2004), presiding over macroeconomic stabilization, constitutional amendments introducing direct presidential elections, the establishment of the KPK, a renewed military offensive under martial law in Aceh, and the controversial issuance of Release and Discharge certificates (SKL) granting legal immunity to major BLBI bank debtors. Despite losing the 2004 and 2009 presidential elections to her former security minister Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono, she remains the undisputed matriarch of Indonesian party politics as lifelong Chairwoman of PDI-P and Chair of the Steering Committee of the Pancasila Ideology Development Agency (BPIP).",
	"lib.pres_004.subtitle": "b. 1947, Yogyakarta — Chairwoman of PDI-P & 5th President of the Republic of Indonesia (2001–2004)",
	"lib.pres_004.term": "Megawati Sukarnoputri",
	"lib.pres_005.alt": "Look back at the ten-year anesthesia of Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono's presidency and ask yourself what a polished voice, melancholy pop albums, and choreographed press conferences actually bought this country besides a decade of cowardly paralysis. In 1998, as the smooth-talking architect of ABRI's 'New Paradigm' ([i]Paradigma Baru[/i]), SBY ran the ultimate institutional con job. Trading away a handful of parliamentary seats so the Army could preserve its lucrative territorial command network ([i]Kodam[/i]) and shield every single general from human rights tribunals. Elected in two landslides by playing the wounded victim, he spent ten years in the palace paralyzed by his obsession with his own approval ratings. Under his watch, the intelligence masterminds who poisoned human rights lawyer Munir Said Thalib on a Garuda flight walked free while the presidential fact-finding report conveniently 'disappeared,' hardline vigilantes torched Ahmadiyah and Christian churches while his police stood watching, the state bled Rp 6.7 trillion in the murky Bank Century bailout, and SBY went on television to sigh [i]\"Saya prihatin\"[/i] ('I am concerned') while the top leadership of his own Democratic Party looted the treasury blind in the Hambalang corruption scandal.",
	"lib.pres_005.desc": "Born in Pacitan, East Java, in 1949, Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono graduated at the top of his Military Academy class in 1973 and married Kristiani Herrawati ('Ani'), daughter of feared RPKAD commando commander Sarwo Edhie Wibowo who led the 1965–1966 purges. Educated at US military staff colleges, Yudhoyono became the public face of the Army's intellectual reform wing in 1998–1999 while serving as ABRI Chief of Sociopolitical Affairs ([i]Kassospol[/i]) and later Chief of Territorial Affairs ([i]Kaster[/i]) under General Wiranto.\n\nSitting on the August 1998 Officers' Honor Council (DKP) that administratively discharged Prabowo Subianto, Yudhoyono served as the chief architect of ABRI's Paradigma Baru ('New Paradigm'). Recognizing that public fury after Suharto's fall threatened the military's institutional survival, he engineered a calculated tactical retreat: severing ABRI's formal ties with [color=__FAC_GOLKAR_A__]Golkar[/color], separating the National Police (Polri) from the military command structure in April 1999, and accepting a phased reduction of unelected legislative seats in exchange for preserving the Army's village-level territorial command network ([i]Kodam[/i]) and insulating the officer corps from human rights courts.\n\nEntering civilian government in October 1999, Yudhoyono served as Minister of Mines and Energy and later Coordinating Minister for Political and Security Affairs under both Gus Dur and Megawati. After skilfully turning a public falling-out with Megawati's inner circle into grassroots sympathy in early 2004, he ran on the ticket of his newly founded Democratic Party and won Indonesia's first direct presidential election in a landslide. Serving two full terms (2004–2014), he oversaw the landmark 2005 Helsinki Peace Accord ending the thirty-year insurgency in Aceh and rode the 2000s global commodity boom, before his second term was battered by chronic policy hesitation, the Bank Century bailout inquiry, and the Hambalang graft convictions that decimated his party's top leadership.",
	"lib.pres_005.subtitle": "b. 1949, Pacitan, East Java — ABRI Chief of Sociopolitical Affairs (1998–1999) & 6th President of the Republic of Indonesia (2004–2014)",
	"lib.pres_005.term": "Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono (SBY)",
	"lib.pres_006.alt": "Ask yourself how it felt to march, donate, and weep in 2014 for the humble carpenter's son in the rolled-up white sleeves only to watch Joko Widodo methodically strangle every single Reformasi institution that made his rise possible. You elected him, we elected him, as the antidote to the oligarchs and the New Order generals; instead, he packed his cabinet with both, bought off the entire parliament into a bloated super-coalition that erased opposition oversight, and in September 2019 gutted the Corruption Eradication Commission (KPK) while students were beaten and tear-gassed in the streets. Behind the folksy [i]blusukan[/i] smile and endless ribbons cut on toll roads and the debt-soaked Nusantara (IKN) vanity capital in the Kalimantan jungle, Jokowi hollowed out Indonesian democracy from the inside. In October 2023, when two terms were not enough for his bloodline, he let his own brother-in-law, Chief Justice Anwar Usman, rig the Constitutional Court's age rules so his thirty-six-year-old son Gibran could ride into the Vice Presidency on the ticket of Prabowo Subianto: betraying the party that made him and handing the republic straight back to the ghosts of 1998.",
	"lib.pres_006.desc": "Born in Surakarta (Solo), Central Java, in 1961 as the son of a small-scale timber seller, Joko Widodo—popularly known as Jokowi—studied forestry at Gadjah Mada University in Yogyakarta before building a furniture export business in his hometown. Completely outside Jakarta's military, bureaucratic, and party oligarchies during the 1998–1999 transition, his political ascent was the ultimate product of the post-1999 Regional Autonomy ([i]Otonomi Daerah[/i]) and direct local election ([i]Pilkada[/i]) reforms initiated after Suharto's fall.\n\nElected Mayor of Surakarta in 2005 on the [color=__FAC_PDIP__]PDI-P[/color] ticket, Jokowi won national acclaim for his unannounced neighborhood inspections ([i]blusukan[/i]), bureaucratic cleanups, and peaceful relocation of street vendors. Propelled by Megawati Sukarnoputri's party into the Jakarta governorship in 2012, he defeated former general Prabowo Subianto in the bitterly polarized 2014 and 2019 presidential elections to become the first Indonesian President without roots in the New Order political or military elite.\n\nOver two terms in office (2014–2024), Jokowi spearheaded a massive infrastructure drive across the archipelago, banned raw mineral exports to force domestic nickel smelting, and launched the multibillion-dollar Nusantara (IKN) capital city project in East Kalimantan. At the same time, he absorbed former rivals—including Prabowo and Golkar—into a dominant executive coalition, signed the 2019 KPK Law revision that crippled Indonesia's anti-graft commission, and pushed through the controversial Omnibus Job Creation Law. In late 2023, breaking openly with Megawati and PDI-P, he threw his state patronage network behind his two-time rival Prabowo Subianto after the Constitutional Court—chaired by Jokowi's brother-in-law Anwar Usman—carved out an age exception allowing Jokowi's eldest son, Gibran Rakabuming Raka, to run and win as Prabowo's Vice President in February 2024.",
	"lib.pres_006.subtitle": "b. 1961, Surakarta, Central Java — 7th President of the Republic of Indonesia (2014–2024)",
	"lib.pres_006.term": "Joko Widodo (Jokowi)",
	"lib.pres_007.alt": "Tell the mothers in black who have stood in the rain outside Merdeka Palace every single Thursday for nearly twenty-eight years (Aksi Kamisan) that a cartoon avatar and a clumsy [i]gemoy[/i] TikTok dance were all it took for you to erase the thirteen student and labor activists who never came home from Kopassus cells in 1998. You watched the man who married into Jalan Cendana, commanded the red berets whose Tim Mawar kidnappers electrocuted pro-democracy students at Cijantung, and was stripped of his Kostrad command on May 22, 1998 after deploying armed troops around Habibie's residence, walk right through the front gates of the presidency without ever facing a court-martial. And what did he do once you handed him the republic in 2024? He appointed convicted Tim Mawar abductors to run your customs service, your cyber intelligence agency, and your provinces; pushed active-duty [color=__FAC_ABRI__]TNI officers[/color] back into civilian bureaucracy; and stood inside Istana Negara on November 10, 2025 to pin the medal of National Hero onto the corpse of his former father-in-law Suharto—proving once and for all that the New Order never died in May 1998, it just waited for you to forget.",
	"lib.pres_007.desc": "Born in Jakarta in 1951 as the son of prominent economist and former cabinet minister Sumitro Djojohadikusumo, Prabowo Subianto spent much of his youth in exile in Europe and Malaysia before graduating from the Indonesian Military Academy in 1974. Marrying Suharto's daughter Siti Hediati Hariyadi ('Titiek') in 1983, he built a fierce reputation as a counter-insurgency field officer in East Timor and rose rapidly under his father-in-law's patronage to command the elite special forces (Kopassus, 1995–1998) and the 35,000-troop Army Strategic Reserve (Kostrad) in March 1998.\n\nIn the final months of the New Order, Kopassus Group IV's covert Tim Mawar ('Rose Team') squad abducted and tortured more than twenty pro-democracy activists—nine of whom emerged alive from secret cells, while thirteen remained permanently disappeared. During the May 13–15, 1998 Jakarta riots, the government's Joint Fact-Finding Team (TGPF) urged an investigation into Prabowo's attendance at a controversial May 14 meeting at Kostrad headquarters while the capital burned. On May 22, 1998, twenty-four hours after Habibie was sworn in, Habibie and General Wiranto stripped Prabowo of his Kostrad command after armed troops deployed around the presidential palace and Habibie's Kuningan residence. In August 1998, an internal Officers' Honor Council (DKP) administratively discharged Prabowo from military service over the activist abductions, but he was never brought before a court-martial and spent several years in self-imposed exile in Jordan.\n\nReturning to Indonesia, Prabowo founded the Gerindra Party in 2008 and ran unsuccessfully for Vice President in 2009 and President in 2014 and 2019. Although Komnas HAM's 2006 investigative dossier on the 1997–1998 disappearances and a 2009 DPR recommendation for an Ad Hoc Human Rights Court remained permanently stalled at the Attorney General's Office, Prabowo was rehabilitated by his former rival Joko Widodo as Minister of Defense in 2019, awarded an honorary four-star General rank in early 2024, and—running alongside Jokowi's son Gibran while rebranded on social media as an endearing grandfather ([i]gemoy[/i])—won the February 2024 presidential election in a 58.6% landslide to become the eighth President of Indonesia, proclaiming Suharto a National Hero in November 2025.",
	"lib.pres_007.subtitle": "b. 1951, Jakarta — Commander of Kopassus (1995–1998) & Kostrad (1998); 8th President of the Republic of Indonesia (2024–present)",
	"lib.pres_007.term": "Prabowo Subianto",
